{"turn": 2, "role": "tool", "result": "**[TERM SHEET --- CONSTRUCTION-TO-MINI-PERMANENT LOAN]{.underline}**\n\n**The Piedmont at Brightleaf** **601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701**\n\n**NON-BINDING EXCEPT AS SET FORTH IN SECTIONS 22 AND 23 HEREOF**\n\n**Date:** January 10, 2025 **Bank Internal Loan Number:** CRE-2025-0342\n\nPrepared by **Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.**, Commercial Real Estate Lending Division, 215 South Tryon Street, 18th Floor, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202.\n\nThis Term Sheet sets forth the proposed terms and conditions under which Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (\\\"Lender\\\") is prepared to provide construction-to-mini-permanent loan financing to Harborview Capital Partners LLC (\\\"Borrower\\\") for the development of the mixed-use project known as The Piedmont at Brightleaf, located at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701. Except as expressly set forth in Sections 22 and 23 hereof, this Term Sheet does not constitute a binding commitment or obligation on the part of the Lender to make the proposed Loan or on the part of the Borrower to accept the proposed Loan.\n\n**[Section 1 --- Parties]{.underline}**\n\n**1.1 Lender. Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.**, a nationally chartered savings bank organized and existing under the laws of the United States, regulated by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The principal office of the Lender is located at 215 South Tryon Street, 18th Floor, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202.\n\n**Relationship Manager:** David Forsythe, Senior Vice President, Commercial Real Estate Lending.\n\n**Lender\\'s Counsel: Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP, 411 South Tryon Street, Suite 2800, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202. Lead Partner: Katharine \\\"Kate\\\" Drummond. Associate: Marcus Webb.**\n\n**1.2 Borrower. Harborview Capital Partners LLC**, a North Carolina limited liability company, organized and existing under the laws of the State of North Carolina, with the North Carolina Secretary of State File Number 1923847, formed on March 14, 2019. The principal office of the Borrower is located at 400 West Main Street, Suite 310, Durham, North Carolina 27701.\n\nThe membership interests in the Borrower are held as follows:\n\n> • **Elena Vasquez-Torres** --- 62% membership interest (Managing Member)\n>\n> • **Brightleaf Investors Group LLC** --- 28% membership interest (a North Carolina limited liability company; NC Secretary of State File No. 2045612; Managing Member: Thomas R. Chen)\n>\n> • **Reginald K. Osei** --- 10% membership interest\n\n**Borrower\\'s Counsel:** Calloway & Strauss PLLC, 324 Blackwell Street, Suite 200, Durham, North Carolina 27701. Lead Partner: Jonathan F. Ngo.\n\n**1.3 Guarantors.** The following individuals shall serve as guarantors of the Loan (each, a \\\"Guarantor\\\" and collectively, the \\\"Guarantors\\\"):\n\n> • **Elena Vasquez-Torres** --- individual, resident of Durham, North Carolina. Ms. Vasquez-Torres has approximately 22 years of commercial real estate development experience. Personal net worth: approximately \\$29,800,000.\n>\n> • **Reginald K. Osei** --- individual, resident of Chapel Hill, North Carolina. Mr. Osei has approximately 15 years of real estate finance and development experience. Personal net worth: approximately \\$11,500,000.\n\nCombined guarantor net worth: approximately \\$41,300,000.\n\n**1.4 Mezzanine Lender (Acknowledged)**\n\nThe Lender acknowledges that **Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC**, a North Carolina limited liability company (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), will provide subordinate mezzanine financing in the amount of **\\$9,200,000** in connection with the Project. The mezzanine loan shall be secured by a pledge of 100% of the membership interests in the Borrower. The mezzanine loan shall bear interest at a fixed rate of 12.50% per annum. The maturity date of the mezzanine loan shall be coterminous with the maturity of the senior Loan described herein, including any extension period, if applicable.\n\nAn intercreditor agreement between the Lender and the Mezzanine Lender is currently under negotiation and must be executed prior to or simultaneously with the closing of the Loan.\n\n**[Section 2 --- Project Description]{.underline}**\n\n**Project Name:** The Piedmont at Brightleaf\n\n**Property Address:** 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701\n\n**Parcel IDs:** 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102\n\n**Total Land Area:** 3.47 acres (combined two parcels)\n\n**Zoning:** MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown). The proposed development is permitted as of right under the current zoning designation. No special use permit, conditional zoning, or variance is required.\n\n**Current Condition:** Vacant improved land. The former tobacco warehouse structures on the site were demolished in August 2024. Environmental remediation of the site was completed in September 2024 in accordance with the applicable directives of the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\").\n\n**Development Description:** The Project consists of the ground-up construction of a six (6)-story (five residential stories over one podium level), Class A mixed-use residential and retail development, together with an associated structured parking garage. The development program is summarized as follows:\n\n> • **Residential Unit Count:** 212 units, consisting of 142 one-bedroom units, 56 two-bedroom units, and 14 three-bedroom units\n>\n> • **Retail Space:** 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail space across seven (7) retail bays\n>\n> • **Parking:** Four (4)-level structured parking garage with 276 spaces\n>\n> • **Total Gross Building Area:** 287,500 square feet\n>\n> • **Amenities:** Rooftop terrace, fitness center, co-working lounge, pool deck, and dog park\n\n**General Contractor:** Graystone Construction Group Inc., 1200 Fayetteville Road, Raleigh, North Carolina 27603 (NC General Contractor License No. 78442; President: Mark D. Sullivan). The construction contract is a guaranteed maximum price (\\\"GMP\\\") contract with a GMP amount of **\\$34,750,000**.\n\n**Project Architect:** Meridian Design Collective PC, a North Carolina professional corporation.\n\n**Projected Substantial Completion Date:** August 31, 2027.\n\n**[Section 3 --- Loan Terms]{.underline}**\n\n**3.1 Loan Amount**\n\n**\\$47,500,000** (Forty-Seven Million Five Hundred Thousand and 00/100 Dollars).\n\n**Loan Type:** Construction-to-mini-permanent loan. The Loan shall be structured as a single advance facility, with loan proceeds to be disbursed in accordance with the approved draw schedule set forth in Section 9 of this Term Sheet.\n\n**3.2 Loan Term**\n\n**Construction Period:** Thirty (30) months from the date of closing. Target closing date: February 28, 2025. Construction Maturity Date: August 28, 2027.\n\n**Mini-Perm Period:** Twelve (12) months following the Construction Maturity Date. Mini-Perm Maturity Date: August 28, 2028.\n\n**Extension Option:** One (1) six (6)-month extension of the Mini-Perm Period (the \\\"Extension Option\\\"), resulting in an Extended Maturity Date of February 28, 2029, subject to satisfaction of customary conditions to be determined and set forth in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**3.3 Interest Rate**\n\n**Construction Period Rate:** Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25% per annum.\n\n**Mini-Perm Period Rate:** Daily Simple SOFR + 3.50% per annum.\n\n**SOFR Floor:** 1.00%, applicable to both the Construction Period and the Mini-Perm Period. If Daily Simple SOFR is less than 1.00% on any applicable business day, SOFR shall be deemed to be 1.00% for purposes of calculating the interest rate applicable to such day.\n\n**Default Rate:** The applicable contract rate plus 5.00% per annum.\n\nInterest shall be calculated on the basis of actual days elapsed over a 360-day year. Interest on the outstanding principal balance of the Loan shall be payable monthly in arrears on the first business day of each calendar month. During the Construction Period, interest shall be funded from the Interest Reserve (as described in Section 10 hereof) until the Interest Reserve is exhausted; thereafter, interest shall be payable by the Borrower from its own funds.\n\n\\\"Daily Simple SOFR\\\" means the Secured Overnight Financing Rate as published by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York (or a successor administrator) for the applicable business day. Benchmark replacement provisions consistent with the Alternative Reference Rates Committee\\'s recommended best practices shall be included in the definitive Loan Agreement, including customary benchmark replacement triggers, successor rate identification, and spread adjustments.\n\n**3.4 Fees. Origination Fee:** 1.00% of the Loan Amount = **\\$475,000**, payable in full at closing. The origination fee may be funded from Loan proceeds in accordance with the approved Project Budget.\n\n**Exit Fee:**\n\n> • 0.50% of the outstanding principal balance at the time of repayment if the Loan is repaid during the Construction Period;\n>\n> • 0.25% of the outstanding principal balance at the time of repayment if the Loan is repaid during the first six (6) months of the Mini-Perm Period;\n>\n> • No exit fee shall be payable if the Loan is repaid after the first six (6) months of the Mini-Perm Period.\n\n**Unused Line Fee:** 0.25% per annum on the average daily undrawn commitment amount, payable quarterly in arrears, commencing on the first quarterly date following closing.\n\n**[Section 4 --- Appraisal and Valuation]{.underline}**\n\nAn appraisal of the Property has been prepared by **Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc.** (Appraiser: Linda M. Haverford, MAI), dated November 15, 2024, and has been reviewed by the Lender\\'s internal appraisal review department. The key valuation conclusions are summarized as follows:\n\n> • **\\\"As-Is\\\" Land Value:** \\$8,900,000\n>\n> • **\\\"As-Completed\\\" Appraised Value:** \\$72,800,000\n>\n> • **\\\"As-Stabilized\\\" Appraised Value (assumes 95% occupancy):** \\$76,200,000\n\n**Appraiser\\'s Projected Stabilized Net Operating Income:**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Line Item**                                                   **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (212 units × avg. \\$2,245/mo. × 12)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN)               \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income (parking, amenities, etc.)                         \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income**                                      **\\$6,462,000**\n\n  Less: Vacancy & Credit Loss (5.0%)                              (\\$323,100)\n\n  **Effective Gross Income**                                      **\\$6,138,900**\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses                                        (\\$1,262,100)\n\n  **Stabilized Net Operating Income**                             **\\$4,876,800**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Implied Capitalization Rate (As-Stabilized):** 6.40%\n\n**Loan-to-Value Ratios:**\n\n> • LTV (As-Completed): \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$72,800,000 = **65.25%**\n>\n> • LTV (As-Stabilized): \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$76,200,000 = **62.34%**\n\n**Projected Debt Service Coverage Ratio (Stabilized, Interest-Only):** Assuming a SOFR rate of 4.35% plus the Mini-Perm Period spread of 3.50%, the all-in interest rate would be 7.85% per annum, resulting in projected annual interest expense of approximately \\$3,728,750. Based on the Appraiser\\'s projected Stabilized NOI of \\$4,876,800, the projected DSCR is \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$3,728,750 = **1.31x**.\n\nThe Lender\\'s maximum loan-to-value ratio shall not exceed 65% on an as-completed basis.\n\n**[Section 5 --- Sources and Uses / Project Budget]{.underline}**\n\n**5.1 Sources of Funds**\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Source**                                                   **Amount**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------ -----------------------------------\n  Senior Construction Loan (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.)       \\$47,500,000\n\n  Mezzanine Loan (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC)            \\$9,200,000\n\n  Borrower Equity (land previously acquired; see Uses below)   \\$0\n\n  **Total Sources**                                            **\\$56,700,000**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**5.2 Uses of Funds**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Use**                                                               **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Land Acquisition (previously closed)                                  \\$8,200,000\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP --- Graystone Construction Group Inc.)   \\$34,750,000\n\n  Soft Costs (architecture, engineering, permits, legal, accounting)    \\$4,150,000\n\n  Interest Reserve                                                      \\$4,850,000\n\n  Loan Origination Fee                                                  \\$475,000\n\n  Developer Fee (5% of total project cost)                              \\$2,835,000\n\n  Contingency                                                           \\$1,440,000\n\n  **Total Uses**                                                        **\\$56,700,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe land at 601 and 615 Foster Street was acquired by the Borrower prior to the closing of the Loan and is contributed to the Project as the Borrower\\'s equity. The land acquisition was funded from prior capital contributions by the Borrower\\'s members. The Borrower\\'s equity in the form of the previously acquired land will be reflected in the sources and uses analysis as the Borrower\\'s contribution to the Project capital stack. No additional cash equity contribution from the Borrower is reflected in the foregoing budget; however, the Borrower\\'s obligation to fund any cost overruns or interest reserve shortfalls from its own funds is set forth elsewhere in this Term Sheet.\n\n**[Section 6 --- Financial Covenants and Underwriting Requirements]{.underline}**\n\n**6.1 Loan-to-Cost Ratio**\n\nThe maximum Loan-to-Cost Ratio shall not exceed **80%** of Total Project Costs at any time during the term of the Loan. For purposes of this Section 6.1, \\\"Total Project Costs\\\" means the aggregate of all costs and expenses incurred or to be incurred in connection with the acquisition, development, construction, and completion of the Project, as set forth in the approved Project Budget. As of the date hereof, Total Project Costs are estimated at \\$56,700,000.\n\n**6.2 Loan-to-Value Ratios**\n\nThe maximum Loan-to-Value Ratio on an as-completed basis shall not exceed **65%**. The maximum Loan-to-Value Ratio on an as-stabilized basis shall not exceed **65%**. The foregoing LTV ratios are based on the appraisal dated November 15, 2024 by Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. and are subject to reappraisal at any time at the Lender\\'s discretion and at the Borrower\\'s expense.\n\n**6.3 Debt Service Coverage Ratio**\n\nThe minimum projected stabilized Debt Service Coverage Ratio shall be **1.25x**, based on interest-only debt service during the Mini-Perm Period. The DSCR shall be tested at the commencement of the Mini-Perm Period and annually thereafter based on actual trailing twelve-month net operating income. If at any time the DSCR falls below 1.25x, the Borrower shall, at the Lender\\'s election, either (a) make a principal curtailment sufficient to restore the DSCR to 1.25x or (b) fund a cash collateral reserve in an amount determined by the Lender.\n\n**6.4 Borrower Net Worth**\n\nThe minimum net worth of the Borrower shall be **\\$12,000,000**, tested annually. The current net worth of the Borrower is approximately \\$14,200,000, based on financial statements provided to the Lender.\n\n**6.5 Guarantor Liquidity**\n\nThe minimum combined liquidity of the Guarantors shall be **\\$5,000,000**, tested quarterly. Current combined guarantor liquidity is approximately \\$9,300,000, consisting of approximately \\$6,100,000 attributable to Elena Vasquez-Torres and approximately \\$3,200,000 attributable to Reginald K. Osei. For purposes of this requirement, \\\"liquidity\\\" means cash, cash equivalents, and marketable securities readily convertible to cash within thirty (30) days.\n\n**[Section 7 --- Guaranty]{.underline}**\n\n**7.1 Completion Guaranty**\n\nEach Guarantor shall execute and deliver a completion guaranty (the \\\"Completion Guaranty\\\") pursuant to which each Guarantor shall jointly and severally guarantee the lien-free completion of the Project in accordance with the approved plans and specifications, the approved Project Budget, and the construction schedule, on or before the Construction Maturity Date of August 28, 2027. The Completion Guaranty shall remain in full force and effect until the Lender has received satisfactory evidence that construction of the Project has been completed in all material respects, a final certificate of occupancy has been issued by the City of Durham, and all construction liens have been released or bonded off.\n\n**7.2 Carry Guaranty**\n\nEach Guarantor shall execute and deliver a carry guaranty (the \\\"Carry Guaranty\\\") pursuant to which each Guarantor shall jointly and severally guarantee the payment of interest and other carrying costs on the Loan during the term of the Loan. The Carry Guaranty is in addition to, and not in lieu of, the Interest Reserve described in Section 10 hereof. The Carry Guaranty shall apply to interest and carrying costs that accrue after the Interest Reserve has been exhausted or in the event that the Interest Reserve is insufficient to cover such costs.\n\n**7.3 Partial Repayment Guaranty**\n\nEach Guarantor shall execute and deliver a partial repayment guaranty (the \\\"Partial Repayment Guaranty\\\") in the amount of **25% of the Loan Amount** (25% × \\$47,500,000 = **\\$11,875,000**). The Partial Repayment Guaranty shall be joint and several, meaning that each Guarantor shall be individually liable for the full guaranteed amount. The Partial Repayment Guaranty shall be subject to customary \\\"bad boy\\\" carve-out provisions, including but not limited to fraud, misrepresentation, voluntary bankruptcy, unauthorized transfer, environmental liability, and waste, which may convert the Partial Repayment Guaranty to a full recourse obligation. Specific carve-out triggers and the circumstances under which the partial recourse guaranty may be converted to full recourse shall be set forth in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**7.4 Environmental Indemnity**\n\nEach Guarantor shall execute and deliver an environmental indemnity agreement (the \\\"Environmental Indemnity\\\"), unlimited in amount, indemnifying the Lender against all environmental liabilities, losses, costs, damages, and expenses (including remediation costs, fines, penalties, and attorneys\\' fees) arising out of or related to the presence, release, or threatened release of any hazardous substances on, under, or from the Property. The Environmental Indemnity shall survive the repayment of the Loan, the release or reconveyance of the Deed of Trust, and any foreclosure or deed in lieu of foreclosure.\n\n**[Section 8 --- Collateral]{.underline}**\n\nThe Loan shall be secured by the following collateral:\n\n> • First priority Deed of Trust on the Property (Parcel IDs: 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102; both parcels located at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701), encumbering the fee simple interest in the Property, together with all improvements, fixtures, and appurtenances thereto;\n>\n> • First priority Assignment of Rents and Leases covering all current and future rents, revenues, and income from the Property;\n>\n> • First priority security interest in all personal property, fixtures, equipment, furniture, permits, plans, specifications, contracts, licenses, approvals, accounts, and general intangibles related to the Project;\n>\n> • Assignment of the construction contract (GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc.), including all rights, claims, and benefits thereunder;\n>\n> • Assignment of the architect\\'s contract (Meridian Design Collective PC), including all architectural plans, drawings, and specifications;\n>\n> • Collateral assignment of all insurance proceeds and condemnation awards related to the Property and the Project;\n>\n> • Pledge of 100% of membership interests in the Borrower by all members (Elena Vasquez-Torres --- 62%; Brightleaf Investors Group LLC --- 28%; Reginald K. Osei --- 10%);\n>\n> • Such other collateral as the Lender may reasonably require in connection with the Loan.\n\n**[Section 9 --- Disbursement / Draw Schedule]{.underline}**\n\n**9.1 General Disbursement Provisions**\n\nLoan proceeds shall be disbursed in phases tied to construction milestones in accordance with the draw schedule set forth in Section 9.2 below. A **10% retainage holdback** shall apply to all hard construction cost disbursements. The retainage shall be held by the Lender and released only upon satisfaction of the conditions set forth below for Draw 9.\n\nEach draw request submitted by the Borrower must be accompanied by: (a) an updated construction progress report prepared by the Borrower and the general contractor; (b) a third-party construction inspector\\'s certification of the applicable milestone completion; (c) partial lien waivers from the general contractor and all subcontractors for work performed through the date of the draw request; (d) an updated title search or bring-down endorsement from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. confirming the continued first-priority status of the Lender\\'s Deed of Trust and the absence of any intervening liens; and (e) evidence that all insurance coverages required under Section 19 remain in full force and effect.\n\nThe Lender reserves the right to conduct independent inspections of the Property and the Project prior to authorizing each draw, at the Borrower\\'s expense. The Lender shall use commercially reasonable efforts to process each draw request within ten (10) business days of receipt of a complete draw package.\n\n**9.2 Draw Schedule**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Draw**          **Milestone**                                  **Cumulative % Complete**   **Amount**\n  ----------------- ---------------------------------------------- --------------------------- ------------------\n  1                 Closing (soft costs, interest reserve, fees)   0%                          \\$9,475,000\n\n  2                 Foundation Complete                            15%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  3                 Structural Frame --- Level 3                   35%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  4                 Structural Frame --- Topped Out                55%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  5                 Building Envelope Complete                     70%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  6                 MEP Rough-In Complete                          80%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  7                 Interior Finish --- 50% Units                  90%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  8                 Substantial Completion / TCO                   100%                        \\$3,475,000\n\n  9                 Retainage Release                              Post-CO                     \\$3,275,000\n\n  **Total**                                                                                    **\\$47,500,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDraw 1 includes the origination fee (\\$475,000), the interest reserve (\\$4,850,000), and initial soft costs (\\$4,150,000). The retainage release (Draw 9) is subject to receipt of a final certificate of occupancy from the City of Durham and final unconditional lien waivers from the general contractor and all subcontractors.\n\n**[Section 10 --- Interest Reserve]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lender shall fund an interest reserve of **\\$4,850,000** from Loan proceeds at closing (the \\\"Interest Reserve\\\"). The Interest Reserve shall be held in a segregated sub-account of the construction loan (the \\\"Interest Reserve Account\\\") and shall be disbursed solely for the payment of interest accruing on the outstanding principal balance of the Loan during the Construction Period. Interest payments shall be made from the Interest Reserve Account on a monthly basis in accordance with the interest payment provisions set forth in Section 3.3 hereof, until such time as the Interest Reserve is exhausted.\n\nIn the event the Interest Reserve is insufficient to cover interest payments through the end of the Construction Period, the Borrower shall be obligated to fund any shortfall from its own funds within five (5) business days of written notice from the Lender. The adequacy of the Interest Reserve shall be assessed by the Lender periodically, no less than quarterly, during the Construction Period, and the Lender shall notify the Borrower of any projected deficiency.\n\n**[Section 11 --- Conditions Precedent to Closing]{.underline}**\n\nThe obligation of the Lender to close the Loan and make the initial advance of Loan proceeds shall be subject to the satisfaction or waiver of the following conditions precedent, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender and its counsel:\n\n> • Execution and delivery of definitive Loan Documents, including but not limited to the Loan Agreement, Promissory Note, Deed of Trust, Assignment of Rents and Leases, Security Agreement, Completion Guaranty, Carry Guaranty, Partial Repayment Guaranty, Environmental Indemnity Agreement, and Pledge Agreements;\n>\n> • Satisfactory completion of the Lender\\'s due diligence, including review of the following:\n>\n> • Appraisal by Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. (Linda M. Haverford, MAI), dated November 15, 2024, satisfactory to the Lender in all respects;\n>\n> • Phase I Environmental Site Assessment prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.), dated October 28, 2024, and related Phase II investigation results, satisfactory to the Lender;\n>\n> • No Further Action letter from the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\"), dated September 30, 2024, in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender;\n>\n> • Preliminary Title Commitment issued by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (Commitment No. NC-2024-88712, dated December 5, 2024) and issuance of a pro forma lender\\'s title insurance policy in an amount not less than the Loan Amount (\\$47,500,000), insuring the Lender\\'s Deed of Trust as a first-priority lien on the Property, subject only to Permitted Exceptions (as defined in Section 18 hereof);\n>\n> • ALTA/NSPS survey satisfactory to the Lender and the title company;\n>\n> • Execution and delivery of the construction contract (GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc.) and the architect\\'s contract (Meridian Design Collective PC), in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender;\n>\n> • Receipt of all necessary governmental approvals, building permits, and zoning confirmations authorizing the construction and operation of the Project;\n>\n> • Borrower\\'s organizational documents, operating agreement, certificates of good standing from the North Carolina Secretary of State, and evidence of authority (including member resolutions authorizing the Loan and all transactions contemplated hereby);\n>\n> • Evidence of the financial condition of the Borrower and the Guarantors, including current financial statements, personal financial statements, and federal income tax returns for the prior two taxable years;\n>\n> • Evidence of insurance satisfactory to the Lender, including builder\\'s risk insurance (minimum \\$34,750,000), commercial general liability insurance (\\$5,000,000 per occurrence), and umbrella/excess liability insurance (\\$10,000,000);\n>\n> • Payment of the origination fee in the amount of \\$475,000;\n>\n> • Deposit of the Borrower\\'s required equity contribution, if any;\n>\n> • Legal opinions from Borrower\\'s counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC) as to due organization, valid existence, due authorization, enforceability of the Loan Documents, and such other matters as the Lender may reasonably require;\n>\n> • Execution of an intercreditor agreement between the Lender and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender, governing the relative rights and priorities of the senior loan and the mezzanine loan;\n>\n> • Such other conditions, documents, and information as the Lender may reasonably require in connection with the closing of the Loan.\n\n**[Section 12 --- Representations and Warranties (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\nThe definitive Loan Agreement shall contain representations and warranties of the Borrower and the Guarantors that are customary for transactions of this type, including but not limited to the following:\n\n> • The Borrower is duly organized, validly existing, and in good standing under the laws of the State of North Carolina and has full power and authority to own the Property, conduct its business, enter into the Loan Documents, and perform all of its obligations thereunder.\n>\n> • The execution, delivery, and performance of the Loan Documents have been duly authorized by all necessary action on the part of the Borrower and its members, and the Loan Documents constitute the legal, valid, and binding obligations of the Borrower, enforceable in accordance with their respective terms.\n>\n> • There is no litigation, proceeding, investigation, or governmental inquiry pending or, to the Borrower\\'s knowledge, threatened against the Borrower, any Guarantor, or the Property that could reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the Borrower, the Guarantors, the Property, or the Project.\n>\n> • The Property is in compliance with all applicable laws, ordinances, rules, regulations, zoning requirements, and environmental laws and regulations.\n>\n> • The financial statements of the Borrower and the Guarantors previously delivered to the Lender are true, complete, and accurate in all material respects and fairly present the financial condition of the Borrower and the Guarantors as of the dates thereof.\n>\n> • No default or event of default exists under any material agreement, instrument, or obligation to which the Borrower or any Guarantor is a party.\n>\n> • All permits, licenses, and approvals necessary for the construction and operation of the Project have been obtained or will be obtained prior to commencement of the applicable phase of construction.\n>\n> • The Borrower is a single-purpose entity with no assets, liabilities, or business operations other than those related to the ownership, development, construction, leasing, and operation of the Project.\n\nSpecific representations and warranties shall be set forth in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**[Section 13 --- Affirmative Covenants (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\nDuring the term of the Loan, the Borrower shall comply with the following affirmative covenants, in addition to such other affirmative covenants as may be set forth in the definitive Loan Agreement:\n\n> • Maintain the Property in good condition and repair at all times;\n>\n> • Complete construction of the Project in accordance with the approved plans, specifications, and Project Budget on or before the Construction Maturity Date (August 28, 2027);\n>\n> • Provide the Lender with monthly construction progress reports, each accompanied by a certification from a third-party construction inspector engaged by the Lender;\n>\n> • Maintain all required insurance coverages in full force and effect at all times, including builder\\'s risk insurance (minimum \\$34,750,000), commercial general liability insurance (\\$5,000,000 per occurrence), and umbrella/excess liability insurance (\\$10,000,000);\n>\n> • Comply with all applicable laws, rules, regulations, and governmental requirements, including without limitation all environmental laws and regulations;\n>\n> • Maintain the Borrower\\'s status as a single-purpose entity in accordance with the requirements of Section 20 hereof;\n>\n> • Maintain a minimum net worth of \\$12,000,000, tested annually;\n>\n> • Cause the Guarantors to maintain combined minimum liquidity of \\$5,000,000, tested quarterly;\n>\n> • Provide annual audited financial statements of the Borrower and personal financial statements of the Guarantors within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year;\n>\n> • Promptly notify the Lender of any material adverse change in the financial condition of the Borrower or any Guarantor, or of any event or circumstance that could reasonably be expected to result in such a material adverse change;\n>\n> • Not modify the Project plans, specifications, or scope in any material respect without the prior written consent of the Lender; and\n>\n> • Comply with the terms and conditions of the intercreditor agreement with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC.\n\nAdditional affirmative covenants to be included in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**[Section 14 --- Negative Covenants (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\nDuring the term of the Loan, the Borrower shall not, without the prior written consent of the Lender, which consent may be withheld in the Lender\\'s sole and absolute discretion:\n\n> • Incur any additional indebtedness, whether secured or unsecured, other than the mezzanine loan from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (as permitted under this Term Sheet and the intercreditor agreement) and trade payables incurred in the ordinary course of business;\n>\n> • Create, incur, assume, or permit to exist any lien, encumbrance, security interest, or other charge upon the Property or any other collateral securing the Loan, other than Permitted Exceptions and the Lender\\'s own liens;\n>\n> • Sell, transfer, convey, assign, or otherwise dispose of the Property or any interest therein, whether directly or indirectly;\n>\n> • Change, modify, or otherwise alter the ownership structure or membership interests of the Borrower, including without limitation any transfer, pledge, encumbrance, or hypothecation of membership interests, subject to the mezzanine pledge as governed by the intercreditor agreement;\n>\n> • Amend or modify the Borrower\\'s operating agreement in any material respect;\n>\n> • Enter into any transaction with affiliates of the Borrower or any member thereof except on arm\\'s-length terms and for fair and reasonable consideration;\n>\n> • Make any distributions, dividends, or other payments to members during the Construction Period, except for payment of the developer fee in accordance with the approved Project Budget;\n>\n> • Amend or modify the construction contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc. or the architect\\'s contract with Meridian Design Collective PC in any material respect without the prior written consent of the Lender.\n\nAdditional negative covenants to be included in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**[Section 15 --- Events of Default (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\nThe following shall constitute Events of Default under the definitive Loan Agreement (subject to applicable notice and cure periods as described below):\n\n> • Failure by the Borrower to pay any amount of principal, interest, fees, or other charges when due under the Loan Documents, subject to a five (5)-day grace period for monetary defaults;\n>\n> • Any representation or warranty made by the Borrower or any Guarantor in any Loan Document proves to have been untrue or misleading in any material respect when made;\n>\n> • Failure by the Borrower to observe or perform any non-monetary covenant or obligation under the Loan Documents, subject to a thirty (30)-day cure period for non-monetary defaults that are capable of cure; provided, however, that if such cure cannot reasonably be completed within thirty (30) days, and the Borrower is diligently pursuing such cure, the cure period shall be extended for an additional thirty (30) days;\n>\n> • Bankruptcy, insolvency, reorganization, or receivership of the Borrower, any Guarantor, or any member of the Borrower, whether voluntary or involuntary;\n>\n> • A material adverse change in the financial condition of the Borrower or any Guarantor, as determined by the Lender in its reasonable discretion;\n>\n> • Failure to achieve construction milestones within applicable timeframes to be specified in the definitive Loan Agreement (a \\\"Construction Delay Default\\\" shall be deemed to occur if the Project is more than ninety (90) days behind the approved construction schedule at any time during the Construction Period);\n>\n> • Abandonment of the Project by the Borrower or the general contractor;\n>\n> • Failure to maintain required insurance coverages;\n>\n> • Breach of any single-purpose entity covenant set forth in Section 20 hereof or the definitive Loan Agreement;\n>\n> • Default under the mezzanine loan documents or the intercreditor agreement (cross-default);\n>\n> • Any material violation of environmental laws or the issuance of any environmental order, directive, or lien affecting the Property;\n>\n> • Entry of one or more judgments in an aggregate amount exceeding \\$500,000 against the Borrower or any Guarantor that remains unsatisfied, unbonded, or unstayed for a period of thirty (30) days;\n>\n> • Any Guarantor\\'s net worth falls below the minimum threshold set forth in Section 6.4, or any Guarantor revokes, terminates, or disaffirms the guaranty or any obligation thereunder.\n\nSpecific Events of Default, together with related cure periods, notice requirements, and remedies, shall be fully set forth in the definitive Loan Agreement.\n\n**[Section 16 --- Remedies (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\nUpon the occurrence and during the continuance of an Event of Default, the Lender shall have the right, at its option and without further notice to the Borrower (except as required by applicable law), to exercise any or all of the following remedies:\n\n> • Accelerate the Loan and declare all outstanding principal, accrued and unpaid interest, fees, and other amounts immediately due and payable;\n>\n> • Cease making any further advances or disbursements under the Loan;\n>\n> • Exercise all rights and remedies available under the Loan Documents, including but not limited to foreclosure under the Deed of Trust in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina;\n>\n> • Apply the Default Rate (applicable contract rate plus 5.00% per annum) to all outstanding amounts;\n>\n> • Exercise all rights and remedies under the Guaranty agreements, including the Completion Guaranty, the Carry Guaranty, and the Partial Repayment Guaranty;\n>\n> • Exercise all remedies available under Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code of North Carolina with respect to personal property collateral and pledged membership interests.\n\nAll remedies of the Lender under the Loan Documents are cumulative and non-exclusive. No delay or failure by the Lender in exercising any right or remedy shall constitute a waiver thereof.\n\n**[Section 17 --- Environmental Matters]{.underline}**\n\nThe following environmental assessments and reports have been prepared in connection with the Property and reviewed by the Lender:\n\n> • **Phase I Environmental Site Assessment** prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.), dated October 28, 2024.\n>\n> • **Phase II Environmental Investigation** completed August 2024 by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC.\n>\n> • **No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") Letter** issued by the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\"), dated September 30, 2024.\n\nThe Phase I ESA identified a Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\") consisting of residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. Contamination levels exceed NCDEQ residential soil standards but are below non-residential (commercial/industrial) standards. The NFA letter authorizes commercial use of the affected area (approximately 0.38 acres), conditioned on the installation and maintenance of a soil vapor mitigation system in the parking garage structure to be constructed on the affected portion of the site.\n\nThe Borrower shall comply with all conditions of the NFA letter, including the installation of the required soil vapor mitigation system, throughout the term of the Loan and thereafter for so long as such conditions remain in effect. The Borrower shall provide the Lender with evidence of installation and ongoing maintenance of the soil vapor mitigation system in accordance with the NCDEQ requirements. Any material non-compliance with the conditions of the NFA letter shall constitute an Event of Default under the Loan Documents.\n\n**[Section 18 --- Title and Survey]{.underline}**\n\nA Preliminary Title Commitment has been issued by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (Commitment No. NC-2024-88712, dated December 5, 2024; Escrow Officer: Patricia Langley) for the Property, consisting of Parcel IDs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102.\n\nThe Lender shall receive a lender\\'s title insurance policy in an amount not less than the Loan Amount (\\$47,500,000), insuring the Deed of Trust as a valid first-priority lien on the Property, subject only to the following Permitted Exceptions:\n\n> • Standard printed exceptions, to be removed or modified as required by the Lender;\n>\n> • Utility easement benefiting Duke Energy Carolinas along the western boundary of the Property (20-foot wide);\n>\n> • City of Durham stormwater management easement (recorded in Book 7421, Page 212, Durham County Registry) affecting approximately 0.22 acres in the southern portion of the site; and\n>\n> • Declaration of Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions recorded in Book 6984, Page 1155, Durham County Registry, by the Brightleaf Historic District Association, Inc.\n\nAll other exceptions shown on Schedule B-II of the title commitment, including but not limited to any prior deeds of trust, shall be removed, released, or otherwise satisfied on or before the Closing Date, and the Lender shall receive evidence of such removal, release, or satisfaction in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender and its counsel.\n\nAn ALTA/NSPS Land Title Survey of the Property, prepared by a licensed surveyor registered in the State of North Carolina, satisfactory to the Lender and the title company, shall be delivered to the Lender and the title company prior to closing. The survey shall comply with the current ALTA/NSPS minimum standard detail requirements and shall include such Table A optional items as the Lender may specify.\n\n**[Section 19 --- Insurance Requirements]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower shall procure and maintain, at its sole cost and expense, the following insurance coverages during the term of the Loan:\n\n> • **Builder\\'s Risk Insurance:** In an amount not less than the total hard construction costs (\\$34,750,000), on an all-risk, replacement cost basis, naming the Lender as mortgagee and loss payee as its interests may appear;\n>\n> • **Commercial General Liability Insurance:** With minimum limits of \\$5,000,000 per occurrence and \\$10,000,000 in the aggregate, including coverage for premises liability, products/completed operations, contractual liability, and personal and advertising injury;\n>\n> • **Umbrella/Excess Liability Insurance:** With minimum limits of \\$10,000,000 per occurrence and in the aggregate, providing excess coverage above the commercial general liability policy;\n>\n> • **Workers\\' Compensation Insurance:** As required by the laws of the State of North Carolina, covering all persons employed in connection with the construction of the Project;\n>\n> • **Such Other Insurance** as the Lender may reasonably require, including flood insurance if the Property is determined to be located in a FEMA-designated Special Flood Hazard Area.\n\nAll policies shall name Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. as additional insured and/or loss payee, as applicable. Evidence of insurance in form and substance satisfactory to the Lender shall be provided prior to closing and at each policy renewal during the term of the Loan. No policy shall be canceled, materially modified, or allowed to lapse without at least thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to the Lender.\n\n**[Section 20 --- Single-Purpose Entity Requirements]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower shall maintain its status as a single-purpose entity (\\\"SPE\\\") at all times during the term of the Loan. The SPE covenants, to be fully specified in the definitive Loan Agreement, shall include the following requirements:\n\n> • The Borrower shall hold no assets other than the Property and related Project assets;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall incur no liabilities other than the Loan, the mezzanine loan (as permitted under this Term Sheet and the intercreditor agreement), and trade payables incurred in the ordinary course of business;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall conduct no business operations other than the ownership, development, construction, leasing, management, and operation of the Project;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall maintain separate books, records, financial statements, and bank accounts from those of any member, affiliate, or other entity;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall not commingle its funds or other assets with those of any member, affiliate, or other entity;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall hold itself out as a separate and distinct entity from its members and affiliates and shall conduct its affairs in its own name;\n>\n> • The Borrower shall observe all organizational formalities, including holding required meetings, maintaining required records, and making required filings with the North Carolina Secretary of State;\n>\n> • Such additional SPE covenants as may be required by the Lender in the definitive Loan Agreement, including without limitation the potential appointment of an independent manager or independent director to the Borrower\\'s governing body, whose affirmative vote shall be required for the Borrower to file a voluntary petition for bankruptcy or to take other specified actions.\n\n**[Section 21 --- Additional Provisions]{.underline}**\n\n**21.1 Reporting Requirements**\n\nThe Borrower shall provide the Lender with the following reports and financial information during the term of the Loan:\n\n> • Monthly construction progress reports, accompanied by a third-party construction inspector\\'s sign-off, within fifteen (15) days following the end of each calendar month during the Construction Period;\n>\n> • Quarterly unaudited financial statements of the Borrower, within forty-five (45) days following the end of each fiscal quarter;\n>\n> • Annual audited financial statements of the Borrower, prepared by a nationally or regionally recognized independent certified public accounting firm, within 120 days following the end of each fiscal year;\n>\n> • Annual personal financial statements of each Guarantor, within 120 days following the end of each fiscal year;\n>\n> • Quarterly guarantor liquidity certifications, in a form satisfactory to the Lender, within forty-five (45) days following the end of each fiscal quarter;\n>\n> • Annual borrower net worth certifications, in a form satisfactory to the Lender, within 120 days following the end of each fiscal year;\n>\n> • Copies of all material notices, permits, governmental correspondence, and regulatory communications related to the Property or the Project, promptly upon receipt thereof.\n\n**21.2 Lender\\'s Right to Inspect**\n\nThe Lender shall have the right to inspect the Property and the Project at any time during normal business hours upon reasonable prior notice to the Borrower. The Lender shall engage a third-party construction inspector, at the Borrower\\'s expense, to review and certify construction progress prior to each draw request and at such other times as the Lender may deem necessary in its reasonable discretion.\n\n**21.3 Assignment.** The Lender may assign, transfer, or participate its interest in the Loan, in whole or in part, without the consent of the Borrower. The Borrower may not assign or delegate its obligations under the Loan Documents, in whole or in part, without the prior written consent of the Lender, which consent may be withheld in the Lender\\'s sole discretion.\n\n**21.4 Governing Law**\n\nThe Loan Documents shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina, without regard to its conflicts of law principles. The exclusive venue for any legal action or proceeding arising out of or related to the Loan or the Loan Documents shall be the General Court of Justice of North Carolina, Durham County, or the United States District Court for the Middle District of North Carolina, and each party irrevocably consents to the jurisdiction of such courts.\n\n**21.5 Expenses. The Borrower shall pay all costs and expenses incurred by the Lender in connection with the origination, documentation, closing, and administration of the Loan, including without limitation the legal fees and disbursements of Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP, appraisal fees, environmental assessment fees, title insurance premiums, survey costs, recording fees, construction inspection fees, and all other out-of-pocket costs and expenses. The Borrower shall be obligated to pay the Lender\\'s legal fees and expenses regardless of whether the Loan closes.**\n\n**[Section 22 --- Non-Binding Nature; Binding Provisions]{.underline}**\n\n**Non-Binding.** Except as expressly set forth below, this Term Sheet is a non-binding indication of the proposed terms and conditions of the Loan and does not constitute a legally binding commitment, agreement, or obligation on the part of the Lender to make the Loan or on the part of the Borrower to accept the Loan. All terms described herein (other than the Binding Provisions set forth below) are subject to: (a) satisfactory completion of the Lender\\'s due diligence, (b) approval of the definitive Loan Documents by the Lender and its counsel, (c) approval by the Lender\\'s Senior Loan Committee (if not previously obtained), and (d) no material adverse change in the financial condition of the Borrower or the Guarantors or in general real estate market conditions.\n\n**Binding Provisions.** Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following provisions shall be legally binding upon the parties upon execution of this Term Sheet:\n\n> • **Confidentiality.** Each party shall maintain the confidentiality of this Term Sheet and the terms, conditions, and financial information described herein, and shall not disclose the same to any third party without the prior written consent of the other party, except (i) to such party\\'s officers, employees, directors, members, legal counsel, accountants, and financial advisors who have a need to know such information in connection with the proposed transaction and who are bound by obligations of confidentiality, (ii) as required by applicable law, regulation, or legal process, or (iii) to the Mezzanine Lender to the extent necessary in connection with the negotiation of the intercreditor agreement.\n>\n> • **Exclusivity.** For a period of sixty (60) days from the date hereof (through and including March 11, 2025), the Borrower shall not, directly or indirectly, solicit, negotiate, or enter into any commitment, agreement, or term sheet for alternative construction financing for the Project from any other lender or financial institution.\n>\n> • Expense Deposit. The Borrower shall deposit \\$75,000 with the Lender upon execution of this Term Sheet as a non-refundable expense deposit, which shall be applied toward the Lender\\'s out-of-pocket costs and expenses incurred in connection with the proposed transaction, including without limitation the legal fees and disbursements of Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP. Any unused portion of the expense deposit shall be applied to the Borrower\\'s obligation to pay the Lender\\'s costs and expenses at closing; if the Loan does not close for any reason, any unused portion of the expense deposit shall be returned to the Borrower, net of expenses incurred through the date of termination.\n\nThis Term Sheet supersedes all prior discussions, negotiations, correspondence, and understandings between the parties with respect to the proposed Loan.\n\n**[Section 23 --- Acceptance and Execution]{.underline}**\n\nIf the foregoing terms and conditions are acceptable, please indicate your acceptance by signing this Term Sheet in the space provided below and returning an executed copy to the undersigned, together with the non-refundable expense deposit of \\$75,000 (payable by wire transfer to an account designated by the Lender), no later than **January 17, 2025**.\n\nThis Term Sheet shall automatically expire and be of no further force or effect if not accepted and returned with the expense deposit by **5:00 p.m. Eastern Time on January 17, 2025**.\n\nThe definitive Loan Documents will be prepared by the Lender\\'s counsel, Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP, and submitted to the Borrower\\'s counsel, Calloway & Strauss PLLC, for review and comment. Closing of the Loan is targeted for February 28, 2025, subject to the satisfaction of the conditions set forth herein and in the definitive Loan Documents.\n\nWe look forward to working with you on this transaction. Please do not hesitate to contact the undersigned with any questions.\n\n**LENDER:**\n\nPINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A.\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: David Forsythe\n\nTitle: Senior Vice President, Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\nDate: January 10, 2025\n\n**ACCEPTED AND AGREED:**\n\n**BORROWER:**\n\nHARBORVIEW CAPITAL PARTNERS LLC\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Elena Vasquez-Torres\n\nTitle: Managing Member\n\nDate: January 10, 2025\n\n**[Exhibit A --- Legal Description of the Property]{.underline}**\n\nSee attached legal descriptions for Parcel 0821-04-72-3341 (601 Foster Street, Durham, NC 27701) and Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (615 Foster Street, Durham, NC 27701).\n\n*\\[Legal descriptions to be provided by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. and confirmed by survey prior to closing.\\]*\n\n**[Exhibit B --- Organizational Chart of Borrower]{.underline}**\n\nSee attached organizational chart of Harborview Capital Partners LLC showing the ownership structure, including:\n\n> • **Elena Vasquez-Torres** --- 62% membership interest (Managing Member)\n>\n> • **Brightleaf Investors Group LLC** --- 28% membership interest (NC Secretary of State File No. 2045612; Managing Member: Thomas R. Chen)\n>\n> • **Reginald K. Osei** --- 10% membership interest\n\nNote: Brightleaf Investors Group LLC is under common control with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), the Mezzanine Lender acknowledged in Section 1.4 of this Term Sheet.\n\n*\\[Full organizational chart to be provided by Borrower prior to closing.\\]*\n\n**[Exhibit C --- Project Budget Summary]{.underline}**\n\nSee attached Project Budget Summary prepared by the Borrower. Summary of Total Project Costs:\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Line Item**                       **Amount**\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Land Acquisition                    \\$8,200,000\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP)       \\$34,750,000\n\n  Soft Costs                          \\$4,150,000\n\n  Interest Reserve                    \\$4,850,000\n\n  Loan Origination Fee                \\$475,000\n\n  Developer Fee                       \\$2,835,000\n\n  Contingency                         \\$1,440,000\n\n  **Total Project Costs**             **\\$56,700,000**\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n*\\[Detailed budget and draw schedule to be provided by Borrower and approved by Lender prior to closing.\\]*\n\nCONFIDENTIAL --- This Term Sheet and its contents are confidential and proprietary to Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. and Harborview Capital Partners LLC. Unauthorized reproduction or distribution is strictly prohibited. Bank Internal Loan Number: CRE-2025-0342.\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_t3PB3ORyBEi6xRsjngDr5772", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/executed-term-sheet.docx\"}"}
{"turn": 2, "role": "tool", "result": "**PINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A.**\n\n**SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE CREDIT MEMORANDUM**\n\nCONFIDENTIAL --- INTERNAL USE ONLY --- PRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL\n\n**CONFIDENTIAL --- SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE CREDIT MEMORANDUM**\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Loan Number:**                    **CRE-2025-0342**\n  ----------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------\n  **Date:**                           January 22, 2025\n\n  **Prepared By:**                    David Forsythe, SVP, Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\n  **Reviewed By:**                    **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_** , Senior Credit Officer\n\n  **Approved By:**                    Senior Loan Committee\n\n  **Borrower:**                       Harborview Capital Partners LLC\n\n  **Project:**                        The Piedmont at Brightleaf\n\n  **Requested Facility:**             \\$47,500,000 Construction-to-Mini-Permanent Loan\n\n  **Recommendation:**                 **APPROVAL**, subject to conditions enumerated herein\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThis credit memorandum has been prepared by the Commercial Real Estate Lending group of Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. for consideration and action by the Senior Loan Committee. The information contained herein is strictly confidential and is intended solely for internal use by the Bank\\'s credit and lending professionals. This memorandum contains proprietary financial information, borrower personal financial data, and the Bank\\'s internal credit analysis and risk assessments. Distribution outside of the Senior Loan Committee, the Bank\\'s credit administration function, and the Bank\\'s legal counsel is strictly prohibited without the express written authorization of the Chief Credit Officer. Any unauthorized disclosure or distribution of this memorandum or its contents may constitute a violation of the Bank\\'s information security policies and applicable law.\n\nRight-click to update Table of Contents\n\n**[Section 1: Executive Summary]{.underline}**\n\nHarborview Capital Partners LLC (the \\\"Borrower\\\") has requested a \\$47,500,000 construction-to-mini-permanent loan from Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (the \\\"Bank\\\") for the ground-up construction of \\\"The Piedmont at Brightleaf,\\\" a 212-unit Class A mixed-use residential and retail development located at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701. The subject property comprises two parcels (Parcel IDs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102) with a combined land area of approximately 3.47 acres. The site is currently improved as vacant land, following the demolition of former tobacco warehouse structures in August 2024 and the completion of environmental remediation in September 2024.\n\nThe proposed development consists of a six-story mixed-use building with five residential floors constructed above a one-story podium containing ground-floor retail space and a four-level structured parking garage. The residential program comprises 212 units: 142 one-bedroom units, 56 two-bedroom units, and 14 three-bedroom units. Ground-floor retail consists of approximately 18,400 square feet across seven retail bays. The structured parking garage provides 276 parking spaces, representing a ratio of approximately 1.30 spaces per residential unit. Total gross building area is approximately 287,500 square feet. Project amenities include a rooftop terrace, fitness center, co-working lounge, pool deck, and dog park. The property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown), and the proposed development is permitted as of right under the applicable zoning ordinance.\n\nThe Relationship Manager for this credit is David Forsythe, SVP, Commercial Real Estate Lending. A term sheet was executed on January 10, 2025 by David Forsythe on behalf of the Bank and Elena Vasquez-Torres, Managing Member of the Borrower. The target closing date is February 28, 2025. The Bank\\'s outside legal counsel for this transaction is Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP (lead partner Katharine \\\"Kate\\\" Drummond; associate Marcus Webb), located at 411 South Tryon Street, Suite 2800, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202. The Borrower\\'s counsel is Calloway & Strauss PLLC (lead partner Jonathan F. Ngo), located at 324 Blackwell Street, Suite 200, Durham, North Carolina 27701.\n\nThe Commercial Real Estate Lending group recommends **APPROVAL** of this loan, subject to material conditions including, without limitation, (a) resolution of a loan-to-cost ratio exceedance through funding of an equity escrow deposit, (b) execution of an intercreditor agreement with the mezzanine lender, (c) implementation of single-purpose entity covenants and delivery of a non-consolidation opinion, and (d) resolution of other conditions detailed in Section 10 of this memorandum. The loan as structured presents certain underwriting concerns regarding borrower equity contribution and LTC compliance that must be addressed as conditions of closing. Additionally, this credit memorandum identifies several risk factors and structural issues --- including interest reserve adequacy, affiliate relationships between a Borrower member and the mezzanine lender, conflicting membership interest pledges, environmental use restrictions, and title matters --- each of which requires resolution or mitigation before or at closing. These matters are fully analyzed in Sections 5, 6, 9, and 10 of this memorandum.\n\n**[Section 2: Borrower / Sponsor Overview]{.underline}**\n\n**[2.1 Borrower Entity]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower is Harborview Capital Partners LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company formed on March 14, 2019 and registered with the North Carolina Secretary of State under File Number 1923847. The Borrower\\'s principal office is located at 400 West Main Street, Suite 310, Durham, North Carolina 27701. The Borrower was formed for the specific purpose of developing and operating The Piedmont at Brightleaf and is intended to function as a single-purpose entity (\\\"SPE\\\") in connection with the proposed financing.\n\nThe Borrower\\'s current net worth, as reflected in its most recent financial statements provided to the Bank, is approximately \\$14,200,000. The proposed loan documentation will include a minimum net worth covenant of \\$12,000,000, to be tested annually within 120 days of the Borrower\\'s fiscal year end. The current net worth provides a cushion of approximately \\$2,200,000 above the covenant minimum.\n\nThe Borrower\\'s principal, Elena Vasquez-Torres, and her affiliated entities have a demonstrated track record in commercial real estate development in the Research Triangle area. Prior to the formation of the Borrower, Ms. Vasquez-Torres and her development team successfully completed four mixed-use projects in the Triangle area, comprising a total of approximately 580 residential units and 65,000 square feet of commercial space, with aggregate development costs of approximately \\$128 million. Each of these projects was completed on time and within budget, and all four have achieved stabilized occupancy levels consistent with or exceeding original projections.\n\nThe Bank\\'s underwriting standards for construction loans require standard SPE covenants, including separateness covenants, restrictions on commingling of funds with affiliates, and prohibition on incurrence of additional indebtedness outside the approved capital structure. Given the affiliate relationship between a Borrower member (Brightleaf Investors Group LLC) and the mezzanine lender (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC), outside counsel should evaluate whether an independent manager requirement and/or a non-consolidation opinion should be required. This is discussed further in Section 9 (Risk Factors). The Borrower\\'s operating agreement will need to be amended prior to closing to incorporate the required SPE provisions and the independent manager requirement, and the Bank\\'s counsel should review the amended operating agreement to confirm compliance with the Bank\\'s standard form and requirements.\n\n**[2.2 Membership / Organizational Structure]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower\\'s membership structure, as set forth in its operating agreement dated March 14, 2019 (as amended), is as follows:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Member**                       **Membership Interest**   **Role**\n  -------------------------------- ------------------------- -----------------------\n  Elena Vasquez-Torres             62%                       Managing Member\n\n  Brightleaf Investors Group LLC   28%                       Member\n\n  Reginald K. Osei                 10%                       Member\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Elena Vasquez-Torres** serves as the Managing Member of the Borrower and has day-to-day management authority over all operations of the Borrower and the Project pursuant to the operating agreement. Ms. Vasquez-Torres has the authority to execute contracts, manage development activities, and conduct the ordinary business affairs of the Borrower without the consent of the other members. Major decisions --- including the incurrence of indebtedness, sale or disposition of the Project, refinancing of existing debt, and any voluntary filing for bankruptcy or insolvency protection --- require the affirmative approval of members holding at least 75% of the outstanding membership interests.\n\n**Brightleaf Investors Group LLC** is a North Carolina limited liability company (NC Secretary of State File No. 2045612) that serves as the investment vehicle for a group of local high-net-worth investors. Its Managing Member is Thomas R. Chen. Notably, Thomas R. Chen also controls Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), the entity providing the \\$9,200,000 mezzanine loan for the Project. These two entities --- Brightleaf Investors Group LLC and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC --- share common control through Mr. Chen, creating a structural affiliate relationship that is discussed extensively in Sections 4.4 and 9.3 of this memorandum. This common control raises concerns regarding the independence of the mezzanine financing from the Borrower\\'s equity structure and introduces substantive consolidation risk that must be addressed through appropriate SPE covenants, independent manager provisions, and a non-consolidation opinion.\n\n**Reginald K. Osei** holds a 10% membership interest in the Borrower and participates as a passive investor and guarantor. Mr. Osei has 15 years of experience in real estate finance and development and contributes financial capacity to the guaranty structure.\n\n**Land Contribution.** The land comprising the Project site was acquired by Ms. Vasquez-Torres on behalf of the Borrower through capital calls from all three members, including Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, at a total acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000. The land was contributed to the Borrower and is currently owned by the Borrower free and clear of liens, except for an unsatisfied deed of trust from Triangle Commercial Bank that is to be paid off at closing (see Section 9.7). The Bank notes that the land acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000 was funded through capital calls from members, including Brightleaf Investors Group LLC. Given that Brightleaf Investors Group LLC is an affiliate of the mezzanine lender, a portion of the \\\"equity\\\" invested in the land may be indirectly sourced from the same investor group providing the mezzanine financing. The Bank\\'s underwriting policy does not count mezzanine debt as borrower equity. Outside counsel should evaluate whether the land contribution satisfies the Bank\\'s minimum equity requirements or whether circular funding concerns exist. This matter is discussed further in Sections 5.1 and 9.1.\n\n**[2.3 Guarantor Profiles]{.underline}**\n\n**2.3.1 Elena Vasquez-Torres**\n\nElena Vasquez-Torres, age 54, is a resident of Durham, North Carolina with over 22 years of commercial real estate development experience in the Research Triangle region. Ms. Vasquez-Torres serves as the Managing Member of the Borrower, holding a 62% membership interest. She has direct oversight of all aspects of the Project\\'s development and will serve as the primary point of contact for the Bank throughout the construction and mini-perm periods.\n\nMs. Vasquez-Torres\\'s personal financial summary, based on the personal financial statement dated December 15, 2024 provided to the Bank, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                    **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Liquid Assets (cash, marketable securities)                     \\$6,100,000\n\n  Real Estate Holdings                                            \\$19,400,000\n\n  Other Assets (business interests, retirement accounts, other)   \\$4,300,000\n\n  **Total Net Worth**                                             **\\$29,800,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMs. Vasquez-Torres will provide a completion guaranty, a carry guaranty, and a 25% partial repayment guaranty (25% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000). The guaranty includes \\\"bad boy\\\" carve-out provisions that convert the partial repayment guaranty to full recourse upon the occurrence of specified triggering events, including fraud or intentional misrepresentation, voluntary bankruptcy filing, unauthorized transfers of collateral, environmental liability, waste, and violation of SPE covenants.\n\n**2.3.2 Reginald K. Osei**\n\nReginald K. Osei, age 47, is a resident of Chapel Hill, North Carolina with approximately 15 years of experience in real estate finance and development. Mr. Osei holds a 10% membership interest in the Borrower and participates as a financial partner and guarantor.\n\nMr. Osei\\'s personal financial summary, based on the personal financial statement dated December 18, 2024 provided to the Bank, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                    **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Liquid Assets (cash, marketable securities)                     \\$3,200,000\n\n  Real Estate Holdings                                            \\$6,800,000\n\n  Other Assets (business interests, retirement accounts, other)   \\$1,500,000\n\n  **Total Net Worth**                                             **\\$11,500,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMr. Osei will provide a completion guaranty, a carry guaranty, and a 25% partial repayment guaranty (25% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000). His guaranty structure mirrors that of Ms. Vasquez-Torres, including the same \\\"bad boy\\\" carve-out provisions converting the partial repayment guaranty to full recourse upon the occurrence of triggering events.\n\n**2.3.3 Combined Guarantor Analysis**\n\nThe combined financial strength of the two guarantors is summarized as follows:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                            **Amount / Ratio**\n  ------------------------------------- --------------------------------------------\n  Combined Net Worth                    \\$41,300,000 (\\$29,800,000 + \\$11,500,000)\n\n  Combined Liquidity                    \\$9,300,000 (\\$6,100,000 + \\$3,200,000)\n\n  Minimum Combined Liquidity Covenant   \\$5,000,000 (tested quarterly)\n\n  Liquidity Coverage Ratio              1.86x (\\$9,300,000 ÷ \\$5,000,000)\n\n  Liquidity Cushion Above Minimum       \\$4,300,000\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nEach guarantor\\'s partial repayment guaranty of \\$11,875,000 represents approximately 39.8% of Ms. Vasquez-Torres\\'s net worth and approximately 103.3% of Mr. Osei\\'s net worth. The Bank notes that Mr. Osei\\'s guaranty exposure is high relative to his personal net worth, and his ability to satisfy the full amount of his partial repayment guaranty in a default scenario is dependent on the value of his illiquid real estate holdings. The combined guarantor financial strength is assessed as adequate but concentrated in illiquid real estate assets. Both guarantors\\' net worth is heavily weighted toward real estate holdings, which could be subject to valuation declines in a market downturn. The liquidity cushion above the covenant minimum (\\$4,300,000) provides a reasonable but not excessive buffer.\n\n**[Section 3: Project Description and Market Analysis]{.underline}**\n\n**[3.1 Project Description]{.underline}**\n\nThe Piedmont at Brightleaf is a proposed Class A mixed-use residential and retail development to be constructed at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701. The Project site comprises two contiguous parcels identified as Durham County Parcel IDs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102, with a combined land area of approximately 3.47 acres. The site is located in the Brightleaf Historic District of downtown Durham, within walking distance of the American Tobacco Campus, Durham Performing Arts Center, and Durham Bulls Athletic Park.\n\nThe site is currently improved as vacant land. The former tobacco warehouse structures that previously occupied the parcels were demolished in August 2024, and environmental remediation activities (relating to petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank) were completed in September 2024. The North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\") issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024 for commercial use of the site, subject to certain conditions described in Section 8.2 of this memorandum.\n\nThe proposed development program is as follows:\n\n> • **Building Configuration:** Six-story mixed-use structure consisting of five residential floors constructed above a one-story podium containing ground-floor retail space and the upper levels of a four-level structured parking garage.\n>\n> • **Residential Units:** 212 units total --- 142 one-bedroom units, 56 two-bedroom units, and 14 three-bedroom units.\n>\n> • **Retail Space:** Approximately 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail across seven retail bays, with street-facing frontage along Foster Street and a secondary entrance from the interior courtyard.\n>\n> • **Structured Parking:** Four-level garage providing 276 parking spaces, representing a ratio of approximately 1.30 spaces per residential unit. Parking will include both assigned resident spaces and unassigned visitor and retail patron spaces.\n>\n> • **Total Gross Building Area:** Approximately 287,500 square feet.\n>\n> • **Amenities:** Rooftop terrace with outdoor seating, gas grills, and fire pit areas; fitness center (approximately 3,200 square feet); co-working lounge with private offices and conference room; resort-style pool deck with cabanas; and enclosed dog park.\n\nThe general contractor is Graystone Construction Group Inc. (North Carolina General Contractor License No. 78442), located at 1200 Fayetteville Road, Raleigh, North Carolina 27603. The president of Graystone Construction Group Inc. is Mark D. Sullivan. The construction contract is structured as a Guaranteed Maximum Price (\\\"GMP\\\") contract with a total GMP of \\$34,750,000. The architect of record is Meridian Design Collective PC, a North Carolina professional corporation. Projected substantial completion is August 31, 2027. The property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown) under the Durham Unified Development Ordinance, and the proposed development is permitted as of right without the need for any zoning variance, special use permit, or rezoning.\n\n**[3.2 Market Overview]{.underline}**\n\nThe Durham, North Carolina multifamily market continues to demonstrate strong fundamentals driven by the area\\'s robust economic base. The Research Triangle region --- anchored by Duke University, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, and North Carolina State University --- has attracted significant investment in biotechnology, pharmaceutical research, information technology, and financial services. Downtown Durham, in particular, has experienced substantial revitalization over the past decade, with the American Tobacco Campus, Durham Innovation District, and the expanding Duke University Health System serving as major employment and demand drivers. Population growth in Durham County has averaged approximately 1.8% annually over the past five years, and the median household income in the downtown core has increased by over 15% during the same period.\n\nAverage market rents for Class A multifamily product in downtown Durham currently range from approximately \\$1,950 to \\$2,500 per month for one-bedroom units and \\$2,300 to \\$3,200 per month for two-bedroom units, depending on unit size, finishes, and amenity package. The Project\\'s projected average rent of \\$2,245 per month across all unit types is positioned in the mid-range of the competitive set and is supported by comparable lease data from properties including the Van Alen, One City Center Apartments, and Beckon on Geer Street. The Bank considers this rent projection to be reasonable and achievable based on current market conditions.\n\nGround-floor retail rents in downtown Durham for Class A mixed-use buildings with street-level visibility range from approximately \\$26.00 to \\$35.00 per square foot on a triple-net basis. The projected retail rent of \\$30.00 per square foot NNN is within this market range and is considered reasonable given the Project\\'s location and visibility along Foster Street.\n\nDurham Class A multifamily vacancy rates have historically ranged from approximately 4% to 7%, with the most recent data indicating a vacancy rate of approximately 5.2% for comparable properties. The Project\\'s underwritten vacancy assumption of 5% is consistent with historical trends and is considered appropriate. While there is a notable pipeline of competing multifamily projects under development or planned in the greater Durham area, market demand continues to absorb new supply at a rate that supports the Project\\'s lease-up assumptions. The Bank\\'s independent market assessment supports the appraised values discussed in Section 3.3.\n\n**[3.3 Appraisal Summary]{.underline}**\n\nAn appraisal of the subject property was prepared by Linda M. Haverford, MAI, of Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc., Raleigh, North Carolina, dated November 15, 2024. The appraisal was prepared in compliance with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice (\\\"USPAP\\\") and meets the requirements of the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989 (\\\"FIRREA\\\"). The appraisal utilized the sales comparison approach for the as-is land value and the income capitalization and sales comparison approaches for the as-completed and as-stabilized valuations. The appraisal conclusions are as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Valuation Basis**                           **Appraised Value**\n  --------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  As-Is Land Value                              \\$8,900,000\n\n  As-Completed Value                            \\$72,800,000\n\n  As-Stabilized Value (assumes 95% occupancy)   \\$76,200,000\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe appraiser\\'s projected stabilized net operating income is \\$4,876,800 per annum, derived as follows:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Income / Expense Category**                                       **Amount**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (212 units × \\$2,245 avg. monthly × 12)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN)                   \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income (parking, amenities, miscellaneous)                    \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income**                                          **\\$6,462,000**\n\n  Less: Vacancy & Credit Loss (5.0%)                                  (\\$323,100)\n\n  **Effective Gross Income**                                          **\\$6,138,900**\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses                                            (\\$1,262,100)\n\n  **Stabilized Net Operating Income**                                 **\\$4,876,800**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe implied capitalization rate on the as-stabilized value is \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$76,200,000 = 6.40%, which is consistent with current market capitalization rates for comparable Class A mixed-use assets in the Durham market. The Bank has reviewed the appraisal and considers the appraised values reasonable and well-supported by the comparable sales data, income approach analysis, and market conditions presented by the appraiser.\n\n**[Section 4: Loan Terms and Structure]{.underline}**\n\n**[4.1 Summary of Proposed Terms]{.underline}**\n\nThe following table summarizes the material terms of the proposed loan facility as set forth in the executed term sheet dated January 10, 2025:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Term**                            **Description**\n  ----------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Loan Amount**                     \\$47,500,000\n\n  **Loan Type**                       Construction-to-Mini-Permanent\n\n  **Construction Period**             30 months from closing (closing target: February 28, 2025; construction maturity: August 28, 2027)\n\n  **Mini-Perm Period**                12 months following construction maturity (mini-perm maturity: August 28, 2028)\n\n  **Extension Option**                One 6-month extension (extending maturity to February 28, 2029), subject to conditions to be specified in loan documentation (see Section 11)\n\n  **Interest Rate (Construction)**    Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25% per annum; SOFR floor of 1.00%\n\n  **Interest Rate (Mini-Perm)**       Daily Simple SOFR + 3.50% per annum; SOFR floor of 1.00%\n\n  **Default Rate**                    Contract rate + 5.00% per annum\n\n  **Payments**                        Interest-only, paid monthly in arrears, during both construction and mini-perm periods\n\n  **Origination Fee**                 1.00% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$475,000, payable at closing\n\n  **Exit Fee**                        0.50% of outstanding principal if repaid during construction period; 0.25% if repaid during first 6 months of mini-perm; no exit fee thereafter\n\n  **Unused Line Fee**                 0.25% per annum on average daily undrawn balance\n\n  **Interest Reserve**                \\$4,850,000 funded from loan proceeds at closing, held in a designated sub-account\n\n  **Retainage**                       10% holdback on hard construction costs, released post-certificate of occupancy\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe interest rate during the construction period, based on the current Daily Simple SOFR rate of approximately 4.35% as of the date of this memorandum, is approximately 7.60% (4.35% + 3.25%). During the mini-perm period, the all-in rate would be approximately 7.85% (4.35% + 3.50%). These rates are subject to change based on movements in SOFR. The SOFR floor of 1.00% ensures a minimum all-in rate of 4.25% during the construction period and 4.50% during the mini-perm period, regardless of declines in SOFR.\n\n**[4.2 Collateral Package]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed loan will be secured by the following collateral:\n\n1\\. **First Priority Deed of Trust** on the Property, encumbering both parcels (0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102), together with all improvements, appurtenances, and related property rights.\n\n2\\. **First Priority Assignment of Rents and Leases** covering all existing and future leases, rents, income, and profits from the Property.\n\n3\\. **First Priority Security Interest** in all personal property, fixtures, equipment, permits, plans, specifications, contracts, and other intangible property related to the Project, to be perfected by filing a UCC-1 Financing Statement with the North Carolina Secretary of State.\n\n4\\. **Assignment of Construction Contract** --- collateral assignment of the GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc.\n\n5\\. **Assignment of Architect\\'s Contract** --- collateral assignment of the architect\\'s agreement with Meridian Design Collective PC.\n\n6\\. **Collateral Assignment of Insurance Proceeds and Condemnation Awards** --- assignment of all insurance proceeds and condemnation or eminent domain awards relating to the Property.\n\n7\\. **Pledge of Membership Interests** --- pledge of 100% of the membership interests in the Borrower by all members (Elena Vasquez-Torres, Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, and Reginald K. Osei).\n\nRegarding the membership interest pledge, the Bank notes a material conflict: the term sheet includes a pledge of 100% of membership interests in the Borrower as collateral for the senior loan. However, the mezzanine loan from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (\\$9,200,000) is also secured by a pledge of 100% of the membership interests. These pledges are mutually exclusive as first-priority security interests. Resolution through the intercreditor agreement is a required condition of closing. Specifically, the closing must be conditioned on execution of an intercreditor agreement with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC that provides the Bank with a first-priority pledge and the mezzanine lender with a second-priority pledge of the membership interests. The Bank recommends this approach (option (b)) as the preferred resolution. Alternatively, the senior lender could forego the membership interest pledge and rely solely on the real property collateral, or the mezzanine lender could subordinate its pledge entirely. However, option (b) preserves the Bank\\'s maximum collateral coverage while permitting the mezzanine lender to retain a subordinated security position. See Section 4.4 (Mezzanine Debt / Intercreditor) and Section 9 (Risk Factors) for further discussion.\n\n**[4.3 Guaranty Structure]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed guaranty structure is as follows:\n\n**Guarantors:** Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei.\n\n**Completion Guaranty.** Both guarantors will jointly and severally guarantee the lien-free completion of construction of the Project in accordance with the approved plans and specifications and the approved construction budget, on or before the construction maturity date. The completion guaranty obligates the guarantors to fund any cost overruns beyond the approved budget and contingency amounts, and to cause the Project to achieve substantial completion and obtain all required certificates of occupancy.\n\n**Carry Guaranty.** Both guarantors will jointly and severally guarantee payment of all carrying costs during the construction period, including monthly interest payments, real estate taxes, insurance premiums, and other carrying expenses as specified in the loan documentation.\n\n**Partial Repayment Guaranty.** Each guarantor will provide a 25% partial repayment guaranty --- 25% of the loan amount of \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000 per guarantor. The partial repayment guaranty is a payment guaranty obligating each guarantor to pay the Bank up to \\$11,875,000 in the event of a payment default or other event of default under the loan.\n\n**\\\"Bad Boy\\\" Carve-Outs.** The partial repayment guaranty will convert to a full recourse guaranty (100% of the outstanding principal balance plus accrued interest, fees, and costs) upon the occurrence of specified triggering events, including: fraud or intentional misrepresentation by the Borrower or any guarantor; voluntary filing for bankruptcy, reorganization, or insolvency protection by the Borrower; unauthorized transfers or encumbrances of the Property or membership interests; environmental contamination caused by the Borrower; violation of SPE covenants; and waste or destruction of the collateral. All guaranties will survive any foreclosure, deed-in-lieu of foreclosure, or other realization upon the collateral.\n\n**North Carolina Considerations. The Bank notes that North Carolina is a judicial foreclosure state and that deficiency judgments following foreclosure are subject to the fair price confirmation hearing requirements of N.C.G.S. § 45-21.36. The Bank\\'s outside counsel (Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP) should review the guaranty structure for compliance with North Carolina law, including the enforceability of the guaranty obligations independent of the underlying indebtedness and the effect of anti-deficiency protections on guarantor liability.**\n\n**[4.4 Mezzanine Debt / Intercreditor Arrangement]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower has arranged mezzanine financing from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), to supplement the senior construction loan and fund a portion of the Project\\'s total development costs. The material terms of the mezzanine financing, as provided to the Bank, are summarized below:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Term**                            **Description**\n  ----------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Mezzanine Lender**                Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC\n\n  **Mezzanine Loan Amount**           \\$9,200,000\n\n  **Interest Rate**                   12.50% fixed per annum\n\n  **Maturity**                        Coterminous with senior loan (including any extensions)\n\n  **Collateral**                      Pledge of 100% of membership interests in the Borrower\n\n  **Payments**                        Interest accrues and is paid-in-kind during construction; cash pay during mini-perm\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe intercreditor agreement between the Bank and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC is currently in the process of being negotiated by the respective parties\\' legal counsel, but has not yet been executed as of the date of this credit memorandum. The intercreditor agreement must be in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel (Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP). At a minimum, the intercreditor agreement must contain the following provisions:\n\n> • The mezzanine lender\\'s pledge of membership interests shall be subordinated to the senior lender\\'s first-priority pledge, such that the senior lender holds a first-priority security interest and the mezzanine lender holds a second-priority security interest in the membership interests.\n>\n> • A standstill period of not less than 90 days following the occurrence of a default under the mezzanine loan, during which the mezzanine lender may not exercise any remedies against the Borrower, the membership interests, or the Project.\n>\n> • Notice and cure rights for the mezzanine lender, permitting the mezzanine lender to receive copies of default notices issued by the senior lender and to cure defaults under the senior loan within a specified cure period.\n>\n> • A mezzanine lender purchase option, granting the mezzanine lender the right (but not the obligation) to purchase the senior loan from the Bank at par plus all accrued and unpaid interest, fees, and costs.\n>\n> • A prohibition on the mezzanine lender modifying any material term of the mezzanine loan (including maturity, interest rate, or collateral) without the prior written consent of the senior lender.\n>\n> • Bankruptcy-related restrictions, including a prohibition on the mezzanine lender filing or joining in the filing of an involuntary bankruptcy petition against the Borrower.\n>\n> • Acknowledgment by the mezzanine lender that it will not contest, challenge, or interfere with the Bank\\'s exercise of remedies under the senior loan documents.\n\n**Affiliate Relationship.** As noted in Section 2.2, Thomas R. Chen is the managing member of both Brightleaf Investors Group LLC (which holds a 28% membership interest in the Borrower) and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (the mezzanine lender). This common control creates a structural relationship between the mezzanine lender and a significant equity holder of the Borrower that must be carefully addressed. The intercreditor agreement should include express acknowledgments of this affiliate relationship and provisions designed to prevent the mezzanine lender from leveraging its affiliate\\'s equity position to the detriment of the senior lender. The Bank does not count the mezzanine loan as borrower equity for purposes of LTC calculation. The mezzanine loan is structurally subordinated debt, not equity.\n\nThe execution of the intercreditor agreement is a mandatory condition precedent to closing and may not be waived. The Bank will not fund any advance under the construction loan until the intercreditor agreement has been fully executed and delivered by all parties.\n\n**[Section 5: Sources and Uses / Project Budget]{.underline}**\n\n**[5.1 Sources of Funds]{.underline}**\n\nThe total development cost of the Project is \\$56,700,000. The proposed sources of funds are as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Source**                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Senior Construction Loan (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.)   \\$47,500,000            83.77%\n\n  Mezzanine Loan (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC)        \\$9,200,000             16.23%\n\n  Borrower Cash Equity                                     \\$0                     0.00%\n\n  **Total Sources**                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**The Borrower is contributing \\$0 in direct cash equity to the Project.** The land acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000 was funded from prior capital calls from members, including contributions from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, which is affiliated with the mezzanine lender, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. All remaining project costs are funded through the senior construction loan and the mezzanine loan. Per the Bank\\'s underwriting policy, mezzanine debt is not counted as borrower equity.\n\nThe LTC calculation presents a compliance issue. Total project costs equal \\$56,700,000. The senior loan amount is \\$47,500,000. Therefore, LTC = \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$56,700,000 = **83.77%**. The Bank\\'s maximum permitted LTC per the executed term sheet and per the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy is 80%. At the maximum LTC of 80%, the maximum permitted senior loan amount would be \\$56,700,000 × 80% = \\$45,360,000. The proposed loan amount of \\$47,500,000 exceeds this maximum by \\$2,140,000. This discrepancy must be resolved prior to closing through the equity escrow mechanism described in Section 5.3 and conditioned in Section 10.\n\nAdditionally, the Bank notes that the Borrower\\'s only equity contribution to the Project is the previously acquired land, which has an as-is appraised value of \\$8,900,000 and was acquired for \\$8,200,000. While the land value exceeds the \\$2,140,000 shortfall, a portion of the capital used to acquire the land was sourced from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, an affiliate of the mezzanine lender. This creates a potential circular funding concern. The Senior Loan Committee requires, as a condition of closing, that the Borrower either: (a) deposit \\$2,140,000 in cash into a Bank-controlled equity escrow account prior to closing; or (b) reduce the loan amount to \\$45,360,000; or (c) provide additional evidence satisfactory to the Bank that the land equity is independent of the mezzanine financing.\n\n**[5.2 Uses of Funds]{.underline}**\n\nThe total development budget for the Project, as supported by the executed GMP contract and the Borrower\\'s detailed budget, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Use**                                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Land Acquisition (previously closed, funded from borrower equity)     \\$8,200,000             14.46%\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP --- Graystone Construction Group Inc.)   \\$34,750,000            61.29%\n\n  Soft Costs (architecture, engineering, permits, legal, accounting)    \\$4,150,000             7.32%\n\n  Interest Reserve                                                      \\$4,850,000             8.55%\n\n  Loan Origination Fee (1.00% × \\$47,500,000)                           \\$475,000               0.84%\n\n  Developer Fee (5.0% of total project cost)                            \\$2,835,000             5.00%\n\n  Contingency                                                           \\$1,440,000             2.54%\n\n  **Total Uses**                                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Reconciliation:** \\$8,200,000 + \\$34,750,000 + \\$4,150,000 + \\$4,850,000 + \\$475,000 + \\$2,835,000 + \\$1,440,000 = \\$56,700,000.\n\nThe developer fee of \\$2,835,000 represents 5.0% of total project costs, which is within the range of market norms for a development of this size and complexity (typically 3% to 6%). The contingency of \\$1,440,000 represents approximately 4.1% of hard construction costs (\\$1,440,000 ÷ \\$34,750,000 = 4.14%), which is adequate for a ground-up mixed-use construction project of this type. The Bank\\'s policy generally recommends a minimum contingency of 3% to 5% of hard construction costs for new construction projects.\n\n**[5.3 Loan-to-Cost Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe loan-to-cost ratio is a critical underwriting metric that measures the Bank\\'s exposure relative to total project costs. The detailed LTC calculation is as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                                               **Amount / Ratio**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Total Project Costs                                      \\$56,700,000\n\n  Senior Loan Amount                                       \\$47,500,000\n\n  **LTC Ratio**                                            **83.77%**\n\n  Maximum Permitted LTC (per term sheet and Bank policy)   80.00%\n\n  **Excess**                                               **3.77 percentage points**\n\n  Maximum Loan at 80% LTC                                  \\$45,360,000\n\n  **Dollar Shortfall**                                     **\\$2,140,000**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe LTC ratio of 83.77% exceeds the Bank\\'s maximum of 80% as stated in the term sheet. The term sheet was executed with a loan amount of \\$47,500,000 and total project costs of \\$56,700,000 without reconciling the LTC covenant. This discrepancy must be resolved prior to closing. The Senior Loan Committee acknowledges the LTC exceedance and approves the loan subject to the condition that the Borrower fund an equity escrow deposit of not less than \\$2,140,000 in cash (from sources other than the mezzanine loan or any affiliate of the mezzanine lender) into a Bank-controlled escrow account prior to or at closing. This equity escrow will be applied to project costs first, ahead of loan advances, to bring the effective LTC into compliance.\n\nFor additional context, the Bank notes the following alternative analysis: If the mezzanine debt of \\$9,200,000 were hypothetically counted as equity (which the Bank\\'s underwriting policy does not permit), the combined \\\"equity\\\" in the capital structure would be \\$9,200,000 and the senior loan LTC would remain unchanged at 83.77%. The combined senior and mezzanine leverage is (\\$47,500,000 + \\$9,200,000) ÷ \\$56,700,000 = 100%, meaning there is zero true equity cushion in the capital structure absent the land value. This analysis underscores the importance of the equity escrow requirement and the need for the Bank to confirm that the land equity is genuinely independent of the mezzanine financing.\n\nEven with the proposed equity escrow of \\$2,140,000, the Borrower\\'s total equity contribution to the Project would consist of \\$2,140,000 in cash plus \\$8,200,000 in land (at cost), totaling \\$10,340,000 out of \\$56,700,000 in total project costs, or approximately 18.2%. While this level of equity is relatively thin for a ground-up construction loan, the Bank takes comfort in the strong LTV ratios (65.25% as-completed, 62.34% as-stabilized) and the combined guarantor net worth of \\$41,300,000 as additional credit support.\n\n**[Section 6: Financial Projections and Underwriting Metrics]{.underline}**\n\n**[6.1 Key Underwriting Ratios]{.underline}**\n\nThe following table summarizes the key underwriting metrics for the proposed loan:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                     **Calculation**               **Result**     **Bank Policy**    **Status**\n  ------------------------------ ----------------------------- -------------- ------------------ --------------\n  LTV (As-Completed)             \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$72,800,000   65.25%         Max 70%            Pass\n\n  LTV (As-Stabilized)            \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$76,200,000   62.34%         Max 70%            Pass\n\n  LTC                            \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$56,700,000   83.77%         Max 80%            **Fail**\n\n  DSCR (Stabilized, I/O)         \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$3,728,750     1.31x          Min 1.25x          Pass\n\n  DSCR (Stress Test, +200 bps)   \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$4,678,750     1.04x          Min 1.25x          **Fail**\n\n  Borrower Net Worth             \\$14,200,000                  ---            Min \\$12,000,000   Pass\n\n  Combined Guarantor Liquidity   \\$9,300,000                   ---            Min \\$5,000,000    Pass\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Projected DSCR (Stabilized, Interest-Only).** The stabilized DSCR is calculated using the appraiser\\'s projected stabilized NOI of \\$4,876,800 and the annual interest expense on the fully funded loan amount at the mini-perm rate. Assuming a Daily Simple SOFR rate of 4.35% (as of the credit memo date) plus the mini-perm spread of 3.50%, the all-in rate is 7.85%. Annual interest on \\$47,500,000 at 7.85% = \\$3,728,750. DSCR = \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$3,728,750 = **1.31x**, which exceeds the Bank\\'s minimum of 1.25x.\n\n**Stress-Tested DSCR.** Under a stress scenario assuming a 200-basis-point increase in SOFR (to 6.35%), the all-in mini-perm rate would be 9.85% (6.35% + 3.50%). Annual interest on \\$47,500,000 at 9.85% = \\$4,678,750. Stressed DSCR = \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$4,678,750 = **1.04x**, which falls below the Bank\\'s minimum of 1.25x. This stress test illustrates the loan\\'s sensitivity to interest rate increases and underscores the importance of the Borrower securing permanent take-out financing or obtaining an interest rate cap prior to the commencement of the mini-perm period or any extension thereof. The Bank should consider requiring an interest rate cap as a condition of the extension option.\n\n**[6.2 Interest Reserve Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is funded from loan proceeds at closing and held in a designated sub-account to cover monthly interest payments during the construction period. The adequacy of this reserve is a critical underwriting consideration, as depletion of the reserve before construction completion would require the Borrower to fund interest from its own resources.\n\n**Current Rate Assumptions.** The current Daily Simple SOFR rate is approximately 4.35% as of the date of this memorandum. The construction period all-in rate is Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25% = 7.60%.\n\n**Full-Draw Scenario.** If the entire \\$47,500,000 were drawn immediately at closing, the annual interest expense would be \\$47,500,000 × 7.60% = \\$3,610,000. Over the 30-month construction period, total interest cost would be approximately \\$3,610,000 × (30 ÷ 12) = \\$9,025,000. However, this scenario is unrealistic because the loan is drawn incrementally over the construction period based on milestone achievements.\n\n**Incremental Draw Scenario.** Based on the draw schedule (Section 7.1), the loan is drawn progressively as construction milestones are achieved. Draw 1 at closing (\\$9,475,000) represents approximately 20% of the total loan commitment. Subsequent draws bring cumulative outstanding balances to progressively higher levels, with Draw 8 (substantial completion) bringing cumulative draws to approximately \\$44,225,000 and the final Draw 9 (retainage release) occurring post-certificate of occupancy. Assuming a roughly linear draw pattern over approximately 18 months to reach the full commitment level (with the remaining 12 months of the construction period at or near full draw), the average outstanding balance over the 30-month construction period is estimated at approximately \\$28,500,000 (roughly 60% of \\$47,500,000).\n\nUsing this average balance, the estimated total interest cost during the 30-month construction period is: \\$28,500,000 × 7.60% × (30 ÷ 12) = approximately **\\$5,415,000**.\n\nThe interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is therefore approximately **\\$565,000 short** of the estimated total interest cost under current rate assumptions.\n\n**Sensitivity Analysis.** If SOFR increases by 100 basis points to 5.35%, the all-in construction rate would be 8.60%. Estimated total interest cost would increase to \\$28,500,000 × 8.60% × (30 ÷ 12) = approximately **\\$6,128,000**, creating a potential shortfall of approximately \\$1,278,000.\n\nFurthermore, the draw schedule is somewhat front-loaded, with the initial closing draw of \\$9,475,000 representing approximately 20% of the total commitment. This front-loading means the average outstanding balance may be higher than the linear assumption suggests, potentially increasing the total interest cost and widening the reserve shortfall.\n\n**Assessment.** The interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is **tight** under current rate assumptions and would be clearly inadequate in a rising rate environment. The Senior Loan Committee approves the interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 as adequate based on current rate assumptions, but notes that this amount is at the lower boundary of adequacy. The loan documentation must include the following protective provisions:\n\n> • A mandatory borrower replenishment obligation requiring the Borrower to fund any interest reserve shortfall from its own funds (not from loan proceeds or mezzanine loan proceeds) upon demand by the Bank.\n>\n> • An interest reserve adequacy test to be performed at each draw request, comparing the remaining interest reserve balance against projected interest costs through the earlier of (a) the next anticipated draw date or (b) 90 days. If the projected costs exceed the remaining reserve balance, the Borrower must deposit additional funds into the interest reserve sub-account before the requested advance will be funded.\n>\n> • A reporting obligation requiring the Borrower to provide the Bank with a monthly reconciliation of the interest reserve balance, actual interest charges, and projected remaining interest costs through construction maturity.\n\n**[6.3 Projected Stabilized Cash Flow]{.underline}**\n\nThe Bank has independently verified the appraiser\\'s projected stabilized net operating income by reviewing comparable market data, lease comparables, and operating expense benchmarks for Class A mixed-use properties in the Durham market. The Bank\\'s assessment of the stabilized cash flow is as follows:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                        **Annual Amount**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (212 units × \\$2,245 avg. monthly × 12)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN)                   \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income (parking, amenities, miscellaneous)                    \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income**                                          **\\$6,462,000**\n\n  Less: Vacancy & Credit Loss (5.0%)                                  (\\$323,100)\n\n  **Effective Gross Income**                                          **\\$6,138,900**\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses                                            (\\$1,262,100)\n\n  **Stabilized Net Operating Income**                                 **\\$4,876,800**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe Bank\\'s independent assessment confirms that the NOI assumptions are reasonable and supported by market comparables. The operating expense ratio of approximately 20.6% of effective gross income (\\$1,262,100 ÷ \\$6,138,900) is within the expected range for a Class A mixed-use property with structured parking, which typically exhibits operating expense ratios of 18% to 24% of EGI. The implied capitalization rate of \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$76,200,000 = 6.40% is consistent with current market capitalization rates for comparable stabilized assets in the Durham market, which generally range from 5.75% to 6.75%.\n\n**[Section 7: Disbursement Schedule and Construction Oversight]{.underline}**\n\n**[7.1 Draw Schedule]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed draw schedule for the construction loan, based on construction milestones and the approved project budget, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Draw**          **Milestone**                                  **Cumulative % Complete**   **Draw Amount**\n  ----------------- ---------------------------------------------- --------------------------- ------------------\n  1                 Closing (soft costs, interest reserve, fees)   0%                          \\$9,475,000\n\n  2                 Foundation Complete                            15%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  3                 Structural Frame --- Level 3                   35%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  4                 Structural Frame --- Topped Out                55%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  5                 Building Envelope Complete                     70%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  6                 MEP Rough-In Complete                          80%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  7                 Interior Finish --- 50% of Units               90%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  8                 Substantial Completion / TCO                   100%                        \\$3,475,000\n\n  9                 Retainage Release (Post-CO)                    Post-CO                     \\$3,275,000\n\n  **Total**                                                                                    **\\$47,500,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Reconciliation:** \\$9,475,000 + \\$5,212,500 + \\$6,950,000 + \\$6,950,000 + \\$5,212,500 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,275,000 = \\$47,500,000.\n\nDraw 1 at closing includes the following cost components: soft costs (\\$4,150,000), interest reserve (\\$4,850,000), and origination fee (\\$475,000), totaling \\$9,475,000. The 10% retainage holdback on hard construction costs is accumulated throughout the construction period and released as Draw 9 upon satisfaction of retainage release conditions (see Section 7.2 below). The retainage amount of \\$3,275,000 represents approximately 9.4% of the hard construction cost GMP of \\$34,750,000.\n\n**[7.2 Construction Monitoring and Controls]{.underline}**\n\nThe Bank will implement the following construction monitoring procedures and draw controls throughout the construction period:\n\n**Monthly Reporting.** The Borrower is required to provide monthly construction progress reports to the Bank, including updated budget reconciliations, schedule updates, and any material change orders or claims. Each monthly report must be accompanied by a certification from the Borrower\\'s authorized representative that no default exists under the loan documents.\n\n**Third-Party Construction Inspector.** The Bank will engage a third-party construction inspection firm (to be selected prior to closing) to independently verify milestone completion and construction progress prior to the approval of each draw request. The inspector will conduct physical site inspections, review construction documentation, and provide written reports to the Bank confirming the percentage of completion and the adequacy of funds remaining in the budget to complete the Project.\n\n**Draw Request Requirements.** Each draw request submitted by the Borrower must be accompanied by the following documentation:\n\n> • AIA Application for Payment (Forms G702 and G703) certified by the architect of record and the general contractor.\n>\n> • Third-party construction inspector\\'s report confirming achievement of the applicable construction milestone.\n>\n> • Partial lien waivers from the general contractor, all subcontractors, and all materialmen for work performed and materials supplied through the prior draw period.\n>\n> • Updated title endorsement (date-down endorsement) from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. confirming no intervening liens, encumbrances, or title defects since the prior draw.\n>\n> • Evidence of continued compliance with all insurance requirements.\n>\n> • Certification by the Borrower that no default or event of default exists and that all representations and warranties remain true and correct in all material respects.\n>\n> • Updated budget reconciliation demonstrating that remaining loan proceeds and borrower equity (if applicable) are sufficient to complete the Project.\n\n**Retainage Release Conditions (Draw 9).** Release of the retainage holdback is conditioned upon: (a) issuance of a final certificate of occupancy by the City of Durham; (b) delivery of final, unconditional lien waivers from the general contractor and all subcontractors and materialmen; (c) delivery of the architect\\'s certificate of final completion; and (d) expiration of the statutory lien filing period under N.C.G.S. Chapter 44A (120-day lien filing window from the date of last furnishing of labor or materials) or, alternatively, posting of a lien bond in form and amount satisfactory to the Bank.\n\n**Equity First.** The Borrower\\'s equity (including the equity escrow deposit, if required per Section 5.3) must be fully invested in the Project before any loan advances are made, other than the initial closing advance (Draw 1). This ensures that the Borrower\\'s equity is at risk before the Bank\\'s capital.\n\n**[Section 8: Insurance and Environmental]{.underline}**\n\n**[8.1 Insurance Requirements]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower is required to obtain and maintain the following insurance coverages throughout the construction period and the mini-perm period, with all policies in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank:\n\n> • **Builder\\'s Risk Insurance:** All-risk builder\\'s risk insurance covering the full replacement value of the improvements under construction, including materials in transit and materials stored off-site. The policy must name Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. as loss payee.\n>\n> • **Commercial General Liability:** Minimum coverage of \\$5,000,000 per occurrence and \\$10,000,000 in the aggregate, naming Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. as additional insured.\n>\n> • **Umbrella / Excess Liability:** Minimum coverage of \\$10,000,000, in excess of the underlying commercial general liability and employer\\'s liability coverages.\n>\n> • **Workers\\' Compensation:** Statutory limits as required under North Carolina law (N.C.G.S. Chapter 97).\n>\n> • **Flood Insurance:** Required if any portion of the Property is located within a Special Flood Hazard Area as designated by FEMA. A flood zone determination will be obtained prior to closing, and flood insurance will be required to the extent applicable.\n>\n> • **Professional Liability:** The architect (Meridian Design Collective PC) is required to maintain professional liability (errors and omissions) insurance with minimum coverage of \\$2,000,000.\n\nAll insurance policies must remain in full force and effect throughout the term of the loan, including any extension period. Certificates of insurance must be delivered to the Bank prior to closing and updated annually or as policies renew.\n\n**[8.2 Environmental Summary]{.underline}**\n\nA Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"ESA\\\") was conducted by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (lead consultant Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.), Greensboro, North Carolina, dated October 28, 2024. The Phase I ESA was conducted in accordance with ASTM Standard E1527-21 and identified one Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\"): residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") located on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The UST was associated with historic industrial operations on the site.\n\nA Phase II Environmental Site Assessment was subsequently conducted in August 2024, which confirmed the presence of petroleum-related soil contamination in the vicinity of the former UST. Contamination levels were found to exceed NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch (\\\"IHSB\\\") residential soil standards but were below non-residential (commercial/industrial) standards. Remediation activities, including soil excavation and disposal of approximately 340 cubic yards of contaminated soil, were completed in September 2024.\n\nNCDEQ issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024, approving the site for commercial use subject to the following conditions: (1) implementation of a soil vapor mitigation system in the structured parking garage constructed over the affected area; and (2) restriction of the affected portion of the parcel (approximately 0.38 acres in the northeast corner) to non-residential use.\n\n**Risk Factor.** The Bank notes that the NFA letter restricts the 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to non-residential use. However, the current project plans position 14 residential units in the building above this area. This potential conflict between the NFA use restriction and the proposed residential use must be resolved prior to closing or, at the Bank\\'s discretion, as a condition precedent to advances for construction above the podium level in the affected area. This matter is discussed in detail in Section 9.5 (Risk Factors).\n\nAn environmental indemnity agreement will be required from both the Borrower and the Guarantors (Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei) as a condition of closing, indemnifying the Bank against all environmental claims, liabilities, and remediation costs arising from the condition of the Property.\n\n**[Section 9: Risk Factors and Mitigants]{.underline}**\n\n**[9.1 Risk Factor: LTC Ratio Exceedance and Zero Cash Equity]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** The loan-to-cost ratio of 83.77% exceeds the Bank\\'s maximum permitted LTC of 80% as set forth in both the executed term sheet and the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy. The Borrower is contributing zero dollars (\\$0) in direct cash equity to the Project. The only equity in the capital stack is the previously acquired land, which has an as-is appraised value of \\$8,900,000 and was acquired for \\$8,200,000. A portion of the capital used to fund the land acquisition was sourced from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, an affiliate of the mezzanine lender, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. The combined senior loan and mezzanine loan totals \\$56,700,000, which equals 100% of total project costs, resulting in zero true ...(truncated)", "tool_call_id": "call_01_ET_AysLXL6R3rESerrxLJtX5764", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/credit-memorandum.docx\"}"}
