{"turn": 5, "role": "tool", "result": "> • **Workers\\' Compensation:** Statutory limits as required under North Carolina law (N.C.G.S. Chapter 97).\n>\n> • **Flood Insurance:** Required if any portion of the Property is located within a Special Flood Hazard Area as designated by FEMA. A flood zone determination will be obtained prior to closing, and flood insurance will be required to the extent applicable.\n>\n> • **Professional Liability:** The architect (Meridian Design Collective PC) is required to maintain professional liability (errors and omissions) insurance with minimum coverage of \\$2,000,000.\n\nAll insurance policies must remain in full force and effect throughout the term of the loan, including any extension period. Certificates of insurance must be delivered to the Bank prior to closing and updated annually or as policies renew.\n\n**[8.2 Environmental Summary]{.underline}**\n\nA Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"ESA\\\") was conducted by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (lead consultant Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.), Greensboro, North Carolina, dated October 28, 2024. The Phase I ESA was conducted in accordance with ASTM Standard E1527-21 and identified one Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\"): residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") located on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The UST was associated with historic industrial operations on the site.\n\nA Phase II Environmental Site Assessment was subsequently conducted in August 2024, which confirmed the presence of petroleum-related soil contamination in the vicinity of the former UST. Contamination levels were found to exceed NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch (\\\"IHSB\\\") residential soil standards but were below non-residential (commercial/industrial) standards. Remediation activities, including soil excavation and disposal of approximately 340 cubic yards of contaminated soil, were completed in September 2024.\n\nNCDEQ issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024, approving the site for commercial use subject to the following conditions: (1) implementation of a soil vapor mitigation system in the structured parking garage constructed over the affected area; and (2) restriction of the affected portion of the parcel (approximately 0.38 acres in the northeast corner) to non-residential use.\n\n**Risk Factor.** The Bank notes that the NFA letter restricts the 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to non-residential use. However, the current project plans position 14 residential units in the building above this area. This potential conflict between the NFA use restriction and the proposed residential use must be resolved prior to closing or, at the Bank\\'s discretion, as a condition precedent to advances for construction above the podium level in the affected area. This matter is discussed in detail in Section 9.5 (Risk Factors).\n\nAn environmental indemnity agreement will be required from both the Borrower and the Guarantors (Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei) as a condition of closing, indemnifying the Bank against all environmental claims, liabilities, and remediation costs arising from the condition of the Property.\n\n**[Section 9: Risk Factors and Mitigants]{.underline}**\n\n**[9.1 Risk Factor: LTC Ratio Exceedance and Zero Cash Equity]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** The loan-to-cost ratio of 83.77% exceeds the Bank\\'s maximum permitted LTC of 80% as set forth in both the executed term sheet and the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy. The Borrower is contributing zero dollars (\\$0) in direct cash equity to the Project. The only equity in the capital stack is the previously acquired land, which has an as-is appraised value of \\$8,900,000 and was acquired for \\$8,200,000. A portion of the capital used to fund the land acquisition was sourced from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, an affiliate of the mezzanine lender, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. The combined senior loan and mezzanine loan totals \\$56,700,000, which equals 100% of total project costs, resulting in zero true equity cushion beyond the embedded value in the land.\n\n**Impact.** The Bank has an outsized exposure relative to total project costs. In a default scenario, the Bank\\'s recovery would depend entirely on the collateral value of the Project and the guarantor resources. The absence of any cash equity contribution means the Borrower has limited \\\"skin in the game\\\" from a cash perspective, which increases moral hazard risk --- the risk that the Borrower may be less motivated to resolve problems or fund cost overruns if the Borrower has no cash at risk. The 100% combined leverage ratio means that any decline in project value below total development costs would result in a loss position for one or both lenders, with the senior lender bearing the risk of value declines below the \\$47,500,000 senior loan balance.\n\n**Mitigants.** (a) The Bank will require the Borrower to deposit \\$2,140,000 in cash into a Bank-controlled equity escrow account prior to closing, which will be disbursed ahead of loan advances to fund project costs and bring the effective LTC into compliance with the 80% maximum. (b) The land has an appraised value of \\$8,900,000, which is \\$700,000 above the acquisition cost, providing embedded equity. (c) The LTV ratios are well within the Bank\\'s policy limits (65.25% as-completed, 62.34% as-stabilized), indicating adequate value coverage. (d) The combined guarantor net worth of \\$41,300,000 provides substantial credit support beyond the collateral. (e) The completion and carry guarantees obligate the guarantors to fund cost overruns and cover carrying costs, providing protection against construction budget and timeline risks.\n\n**Residual Concern.** Even with the equity escrow, the Borrower\\'s total equity contribution would be only \\$2,140,000 in cash plus \\$8,200,000 in land (at cost) = \\$10,340,000 out of \\$56,700,000 in total project costs = approximately 18.2%. This is relatively thin for a ground-up construction loan, where the Bank\\'s policy guidance generally targets borrower equity contributions of 20% or more. However, the strong LTV position and guarantor support mitigate this concern to an acceptable level.\n\n**[9.2 Risk Factor: Interest Reserve Adequacy]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** As analyzed in Section 6.2, the \\$4,850,000 interest reserve funded from loan proceeds at closing may be insufficient by approximately \\$565,000 under current rate assumptions and a reasonable incremental draw assumption. If SOFR increases by 100 basis points, the estimated shortfall would widen to approximately \\$1,278,000.\n\n**Impact.** If the interest reserve is depleted before construction completion, the Borrower must fund monthly interest payments from its own resources. Given the Borrower\\'s zero cash equity contribution, the Borrower may lack immediate liquidity to fund interest shortfalls, potentially creating a payment default during the construction period. A payment default during construction could trigger cross-defaults under the mezzanine loan and could jeopardize the construction timeline if the Bank suspends further advances.\n\n**Mitigants.** (a) The loan documentation will include a mandatory borrower replenishment obligation requiring the Borrower to fund any interest reserve shortfall from its own funds upon demand by the Bank. (b) Interest reserve adequacy will be tested at each draw request, with the Borrower required to replenish the reserve if projected remaining interest costs during the construction period exceed the remaining reserve balance. (c) The guarantors maintain combined liquidity of \\$9,300,000, which provides a backstop for interest reserve replenishment. (d) The interest reserve analysis assumes a current SOFR of approximately 4.35%. If SOFR rates decline during the construction period, the reserve could prove adequate or even generate a surplus.\n\n**Recommendation.** The Senior Loan Committee approves the \\$4,850,000 interest reserve as adequate based on current rate assumptions, with mandatory contractual protections as described above. The Bank should monitor SOFR rate trends closely and communicate proactively with the Borrower regarding any anticipated replenishment obligations.\n\n**[9.3 Risk Factor: Mezzanine Lender Affiliate Relationship and SPE Concerns]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** Brightleaf Investors Group LLC (28% member of the Borrower) shares common control with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (mezzanine lender) through Thomas R. Chen. This common control creates a structural conflict and raises the following specific concerns:\n\nFirst, the mezzanine lender is providing \\$9,200,000 in subordinated debt to a borrower in which its affiliate holds a significant (28%) equity interest. This interrelationship blurs the distinction between debt and equity in the Borrower\\'s capital structure and may complicate the enforcement of remedies in a default or insolvency scenario.\n\nSecond, the affiliate relationship increases **substantive consolidation risk**. In a bankruptcy proceeding involving the Borrower, a court could potentially substantively consolidate the assets and liabilities of the Borrower with those of Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, or other entities under common control with Thomas R. Chen. Substantive consolidation would merge the separate estates into a single bankruptcy estate, potentially subordinating the Bank\\'s claims, complicating the Bank\\'s realization upon its collateral, or exposing the collateral to the claims of creditors of the affiliated entities.\n\nThird, the Borrower\\'s intended status as a single-purpose entity requires strict separateness from its members and their affiliates. Standard SPE covenants mandate separate books and records, no commingling of funds, arm\\'s-length transactions, separate corporate formalities, and restrictions on affiliate dealings. The affiliate structure between a Borrower member and the mezzanine lender creates inherent tension with these separateness requirements.\n\n**Impact.** If SPE covenants are inadequate or are not faithfully observed, the Bank\\'s collateral position could be compromised in a bankruptcy or insolvency proceeding involving the Borrower or any affiliated entity. The risk of substantive consolidation, while relatively uncommon in practice, is heightened in situations where affiliated entities have overlapping ownership, shared management, commingled funds, or inadequate corporate formalities.\n\n**Mitigants.** The Bank requires the following protective measures as conditions of closing:\n\n\\(a\\) **Robust SPE Covenants.** The loan agreement must include comprehensive SPE covenants requiring the Borrower to: maintain its existence as a separate legal entity; maintain separate books, records, and bank accounts; conduct all transactions with affiliates on arm\\'s-length terms; refrain from commingling its assets or funds with those of any member, affiliate, or other person; observe all limited liability company formalities; hold itself out as an entity separate and distinct from its members and their affiliates; and pay its own liabilities and expenses from its own funds.\n\n\\(b\\) **Independent Manager.** The Borrower\\'s operating agreement must be amended to require the appointment of at least one independent manager (who is not an officer, director, employee, or affiliate of the Borrower, any member, or the mezzanine lender) whose affirmative vote is required for any voluntary filing for bankruptcy, reorganization, or insolvency protection. This provision is the single most important structural safeguard against a bad-faith bankruptcy filing.\n\n\\(c\\) **Non-Consolidation Opinion.** Borrower\\'s counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC) must deliver, at closing, a non-consolidation opinion addressed to the Bank, opining that, subject to customary assumptions and qualifications, a court applying applicable North Carolina law would not substantively consolidate the Borrower with any of its members, affiliates, or other related entities. This opinion provides additional assurance (though not a guarantee) that the Borrower\\'s separate existence will be respected in a judicial proceeding.\n\n\\(d\\) **Intercreditor Provisions.** The intercreditor agreement must include express acknowledgments of the affiliate relationship and provisions prohibiting the mezzanine lender from filing or joining in the filing of an involuntary bankruptcy petition against the Borrower.\n\n**Market Standard.** For securitization-eligible construction loans, industry-standard SPE requirements include independent director or independent manager provisions and non-consolidation opinions from borrower\\'s counsel. While this loan is not currently intended for securitization, implementing these protections is prudent given the heightened affiliate risk. The analytical framework for non-consolidation analysis draws on Delaware LLC law principles (even though the Borrower is a North Carolina LLC), as the Delaware case law provides the most developed body of precedent on substantive consolidation in the context of structured finance transactions.\n\n**[9.4 Risk Factor: Conflicting Membership Interest Pledges]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** Both the senior construction loan and the mezzanine loan are secured by a pledge of 100% of the membership interests in the Borrower. As a matter of law and commercial practice, only one lender can hold a first-priority security interest in the same collateral. As currently documented, the pledges are mutually exclusive as first-priority interests, creating an ambiguity that could result in litigation or contested priority in a default scenario.\n\n**Impact.** If the conflicting pledges are not resolved, the Bank may not have the collateral position described in the term sheet. In a default, the mezzanine lender could assert a competing first-priority claim to the membership interests, and the resulting dispute could delay or prevent the Bank\\'s exercise of remedies, including foreclosure on the membership interests or exercise of control over the Borrower.\n\n**Mitigants.** Execution of the intercreditor agreement (currently negotiated but not yet executed) must be a condition precedent to closing. The intercreditor agreement must provide: (i) the senior lender (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.) holds a first-priority security interest in both the real property collateral and the membership interests; (ii) the mezzanine lender (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC) holds a subordinated second-priority security interest in the membership interests; (iii) the mezzanine lender is subject to a 90-day standstill period before exercising any remedies; (iv) the mezzanine lender has cure rights and a purchase option (right to acquire the senior loan at par plus accrued); and (v) the mezzanine lender may not modify the terms of the mezzanine loan without the Bank\\'s prior written consent.\n\n**Alternative.** If the intercreditor agreement cannot be finalized on terms satisfactory to the Bank, the Bank could forego the membership interest pledge and rely solely on its real property collateral (first-priority deed of trust, assignment of rents and leases, security interest in personal property). The LTV ratios support the real property collateral alone. However, the membership interest pledge provides an additional layer of protection and operational control that the Bank considers valuable, particularly in a construction loan context where the Bank needs the ability to step in and control the development entity if the Borrower defaults.\n\n**Recommendation.** The Bank requires execution of the intercreditor agreement as a hard closing condition. The loan may not close without a fully executed and delivered intercreditor agreement in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel.\n\n**[9.5 Risk Factor: Environmental Use Restriction / Residential Conflict]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** The NFA letter issued by NCDEQ on September 30, 2024 restricts the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to non-residential use. However, the current project plans position 14 residential units in the building structure directly above this area at the podium level and upper floors.\n\n**Impact.** If this conflict is not resolved, those 14 units may not receive residential certificates of occupancy from the City of Durham, which would reduce the Project\\'s rental income and adversely affect the Bank\\'s underwriting assumptions. The estimated annual revenue impact of losing 14 residential units is approximately \\$377,160 (14 units × \\$2,245 average monthly rent × 12 months). This would reduce the stabilized NOI to approximately \\$4,499,640 (\\$4,876,800 − \\$377,160), and the recalculated DSCR would be approximately \\$4,499,640 ÷ \\$3,728,750 = 1.21x, which is below the Bank\\'s minimum of 1.25x. Additionally, the as-completed and as-stabilized appraised values may be overstated if they assume occupancy and income from the affected 14 units without accounting for the use restriction.\n\n**Mitigants.** The Bank requires the Borrower, as a condition of closing (or, at the Bank\\'s discretion, as a condition precedent to advances for construction above the podium level in the affected area), to achieve one of the following resolutions: (a) obtain a modification to the NFA letter from NCDEQ permitting residential use of the area, contingent on installation and ongoing maintenance of the soil vapor mitigation system; (b) redesign the 14 affected units as commercial or retail space and provide updated financial projections; or (c) provide written confirmation from NCDEQ that residential use is permitted in the floors above the grade-level restricted area, provided that appropriate engineering controls (including the vapor mitigation system) are in place and operational.\n\n**Recommendation.** The Bank flags this issue for review by outside counsel and environmental consultants. The resolution approach should be documented as a specific covenant in the loan documentation, and the Bank should retain discretion to withhold advances for construction in the affected area until the issue is resolved to the Bank\\'s satisfaction.\n\n**[9.6 Risk Factor: Construction Timeline and Market Risk]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** The 30-month construction period targets projected substantial completion on August 31, 2027. The construction maturity date under the loan is August 28, 2027 --- only three days before the projected substantial completion date. Any construction delay, even a minor one, could push substantial completion past the construction maturity date, triggering a maturity default under the loan.\n\n**Mitigants.** The mini-perm period provides an additional 12 months following the construction maturity date (extending to August 28, 2028), plus the 6-month extension option (to February 28, 2029). These periods provide a substantial buffer for the Borrower to complete construction, achieve stabilization, and arrange permanent take-out financing. The GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc. provides cost certainty (subject to approved change orders), and the contingency of \\$1,440,000 provides approximately 4.1% cushion on hard construction costs. The completion guaranty from both guarantors provides the Bank with recourse against the guarantors\\' personal assets in the event construction is not completed.\n\n**Market Risk.** The Durham multifamily market is currently strong, but the 30-month construction horizon introduces inherent uncertainty regarding supply, demand, and rental rate trends. The 5% vacancy assumption is adequate based on historical trends, and the Bank considers the risk of a significant market downturn during the construction period to be moderate but not negligible. The Bank will monitor market conditions throughout the construction period and may require updated market studies as a condition of the extension option.\n\n**[9.7 Risk Factor: Title --- Unsatisfied Prior Deed of Trust]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk. The title commitment issued by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (Commitment No. NC-2024-88712, dated December 5, 2024, escrow officer Patricia Langley) identifies an unsatisfied deed of trust recorded in favor of Triangle Commercial Bank, dated 2018, encumbering the Property and securing indebtedness of \\$3,400,000. Borrower\\'s counsel (Jonathan F. Ngo, Calloway & Strauss PLLC) represents that this deed of trust will be satisfied at closing from the proceeds of the senior construction loan or from borrower funds.**\n\n**Impact.** If the prior deed of trust is not satisfied and released at closing, the Bank would not hold a first-priority lien on the Property as required under the loan terms.\n\n**Mitigant. Closing is conditioned on receipt of a satisfactory title insurance policy from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. insuring the first-priority lien of the Bank\\'s deed of trust, free and clear of the prior Triangle Commercial Bank deed of trust. The Bank requires delivery of a payoff letter from Triangle Commercial Bank and simultaneous recording of the satisfaction or release of the prior deed of trust with the recording of the Bank\\'s deed of trust. The title insurance policy must be issued without any exception for the prior deed of trust.**\n\n**[9.8 Additional Risk Considerations]{.underline}**\n\n**Brightleaf Historic District Association CC&Rs.** The Property is located within the Brightleaf Historic District, which is subject to Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions recorded in Book 6984, Page 1155 of the Durham County Register of Deeds. The CC&Rs include architectural review and design standards that may affect the design and construction of the Project. The Bank requires, as a condition of closing, evidence that the Brightleaf Historic District Association has reviewed and approved the Project plans, or that the CC&Rs do not apply to the Project or have been waived with respect to the Project.\n\n**Interest Rate Risk.** The loan bears a floating interest rate based on Daily Simple SOFR, which exposes both the Bank and the Borrower to interest rate volatility. As demonstrated in the stress test analysis (Section 6.1), a 200-basis-point increase in SOFR would push the projected stabilized DSCR to 1.04x, well below the Bank\\'s minimum. The Bank should consider requiring the Borrower to obtain an interest rate cap agreement for the mini-perm period and any extension period. The loan documentation must include SOFR benchmark replacement provisions consistent with the Alternative Reference Rates Committee (\\\"ARRC\\\") recommended fallback language.\n\n**Guarantor Concentration in Illiquid Assets.** Both guarantors have personal net worth heavily concentrated in real estate holdings. Ms. Vasquez-Torres\\'s real estate holdings constitute approximately 65% of her total net worth, and Mr. Osei\\'s constitute approximately 59%. A decline in real estate values could erode guarantor net worth and impair their ability to perform under the guarantees.\n\n**General Contractor Risk.** Graystone Construction Group Inc.\\'s GMP contract provides cost certainty for hard construction costs. The Bank should verify the financial strength and payment and performance bonding capacity of the contractor prior to closing. Evidence of the contractor\\'s financial statements and bonding capacity should be included in the closing deliverables.\n\n**[Section 10: Conditions of Approval]{.underline}**\n\n**[10.1 Conditions Precedent to Closing]{.underline}**\n\nThe approval of this loan is subject to the satisfaction of each of the following conditions precedent to closing, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel (Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP):\n\n1\\. **Loan Documentation.** Execution and delivery of all loan documents, including but not limited to the loan agreement, promissory note, deed of trust, assignment of rents and leases, security agreement, environmental indemnity, membership interest pledge agreements, and all other instruments and agreements required by the Bank and its counsel, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank.\n\n2\\. **Equity Escrow Deposit.** Borrower shall deposit not less than \\$2,140,000 in cash (from sources other than the mezzanine loan, any affiliate of the mezzanine lender, or the proceeds of any subordinate financing) into a Bank-controlled equity escrow account prior to or at closing. Such funds shall be disbursed ahead of loan advances to fund project costs, thereby reducing the effective LTC to 80% or below. The source of the equity escrow funds must be verified to the Bank\\'s satisfaction through documentation demonstrating that the funds are not directly or indirectly sourced from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, Thomas R. Chen, or any entity under common control with any of the foregoing.\n\n3\\. **Intercreditor Agreement.** Execution and delivery of an intercreditor agreement between Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel, providing for: (i) subordination of the mezzanine lender\\'s pledge of membership interests to the Bank\\'s first-priority pledge; (ii) a standstill period of not less than 90 days; (iii) cure rights and purchase option for the mezzanine lender; and (iv) bankruptcy protections, including a prohibition on the mezzanine lender filing or joining in the filing of an involuntary bankruptcy petition against the Borrower. The intercreditor agreement must be fully executed and delivered at closing.\n\n4\\. **SPE Covenants and Non-Consolidation Opinion.** (a) The Borrower\\'s operating agreement shall be amended to include comprehensive SPE covenants and an independent manager provision requiring the affirmative consent of an independent manager (not affiliated with the Borrower, any member, the mezzanine lender, or Thomas R. Chen) for any voluntary filing for bankruptcy, reorganization, or insolvency protection. (b) Delivery of a non-consolidation opinion from Borrower\\'s counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC), addressed to the Bank, in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel, opining that a court would not substantively consolidate the Borrower with any of its members, affiliates, or related entities.\n\n5\\. Satisfactory Title. Issuance of a lender\\'s title insurance policy by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (escrow officer Patricia Langley) insuring the first-priority lien of the Bank\\'s deed of trust on both parcels (0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102), in the amount of \\$47,500,000, free and clear of the unsatisfied deed of trust from Triangle Commercial Bank (to be satisfied at closing through delivery of a payoff letter and simultaneous recording of a satisfaction or release), and subject only to approved standard title exceptions as determined by the Bank and its counsel.\n\n6\\. **Appraisal Confirmation.** Confirmation that the as-completed appraised value is not less than \\$72,800,000 and the as-stabilized appraised value is not less than \\$76,200,000, as reflected in the appraisal by Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. dated November 15, 2024.\n\n7\\. **Environmental.** (a) Satisfactory Phase I ESA and Phase II results from Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC; (b) evidence of the NCDEQ No Further Action letter dated September 30, 2024; (c) evidence that the soil vapor mitigation system design is incorporated into the approved construction plans; (d) resolution of the residential use restriction on the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 --- either through modification of the NFA letter, redesign of the affected units, or NCDEQ confirmation that residential use above the grade-level restricted area is permitted with engineering controls. At the Bank\\'s discretion, resolution of the use restriction may be deferred to a condition precedent to advances for construction in the affected area rather than a closing condition.\n\n8\\. **Construction Contract.** Execution of the GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc. with a guaranteed maximum price of \\$34,750,000, together with delivery of a collateral assignment of the construction contract in favor of the Bank.\n\n9\\. **Architect\\'s Contract.** Delivery of a collateral assignment of the architect\\'s agreement with Meridian Design Collective PC in favor of the Bank.\n\n10\\. **Insurance.** Evidence of all required insurance coverages as specified in Section 8.1, including builder\\'s risk, commercial general liability, umbrella/excess liability, workers\\' compensation, and flood insurance (if applicable).\n\n11\\. **Personal Guaranties.** Execution and delivery of completion guaranties, carry guaranties, and 25% partial repayment guaranties by each of Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel.\n\n12\\. **Organizational Documents.** Delivery of certified copies of the Borrower\\'s articles of organization, operating agreement (as amended for SPE covenants and independent manager provision), certificates of good standing from the North Carolina Secretary of State (current within 30 days of closing), and resolutions or written consents of members authorizing the loan, the execution and delivery of the loan documents, and the granting of the security interests.\n\n13\\. **Governmental Approvals.** Evidence that all required governmental approvals, permits, and zoning compliance are in place, including a zoning compliance letter from the City of Durham confirming that the proposed development is permitted as of right under the MU-D zoning district.\n\n14\\. **Brightleaf Historic District Association.** Evidence of Brightleaf Historic District Association approval of the project plans and architectural design, or evidence that the CC&Rs (Book 6984, Page 1155) do not require such approval or that the approval requirement has been waived.\n\n15\\. **Legal Opinions.** Delivery of legal opinions from Borrower\\'s counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC) addressed to the Bank, including opinions as to enforceability of the loan documents, authority of the Borrower and its members, the non-consolidation opinion described in Condition 4(b) above, and such other opinions as the Bank and its counsel may reasonably require.\n\n16\\. **Origination Fee.** Payment of the origination fee of \\$475,000 (1.00% of \\$47,500,000).\n\n17\\. **Borrower Net Worth Certificate.** Delivery of a net worth certificate from the Borrower, certified by the Managing Member, demonstrating a minimum net worth of not less than \\$12,000,000.\n\n18\\. **Guarantor Financial Statements.** Delivery of current personal financial statements from each guarantor, together with verification of combined guarantor liquidity of not less than \\$5,000,000.\n\n19\\. **Additional Deliverables.** All other documents, instruments, certificates, and opinions customarily required for a construction-to-mini-permanent loan transaction of this type and as the Bank and its counsel may reasonably require.\n\n**[10.2 Conditions Precedent to Each Advance]{.underline}**\n\nIn addition to the closing conditions, each advance (draw) under the construction loan is subject to satisfaction of the following conditions:\n\n1\\. **No Default.** No event of default exists under the loan documents, and no condition exists that with the giving of notice or the passage of time, or both, would constitute an event of default.\n\n2\\. **Representations and Warranties.** All representations and warranties contained in the loan documents remain true and correct in all material respects as of the date of the advance.\n\n3\\. **Construction Inspector Report.** Delivery of a satisfactory report from the Bank\\'s third-party construction inspector confirming achievement of the applicable construction milestone and the percentage of completion warranted in the draw request.\n\n4\\. **AIA Application for Payment.** Delivery of a completed AIA Application and Certificate for Payment (Form G702) and Continuation Sheet (Form G703) certified by the architect of record and the general contractor.\n\n5\\. **Lien Waivers.** Delivery of partial lien waivers (in statutory form under N.C.G.S. Chapter 44A) from the general contractor and all subcontractors and materialmen for work performed and materials furnished through the prior draw period.\n\n6\\. Title Date-Down. Delivery of a title date-down endorsement from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. confirming no intervening liens, encumbrances, or title defects since the date of the prior title endorsement (or the original title policy date, for the first advance after closing).\n\n7\\. **Interest Reserve Adequacy Test.** The Borrower must demonstrate that the remaining interest reserve balance, together with any additional funds the Borrower commits to deposit, is sufficient to cover projected interest costs through the earlier of (a) the next anticipated draw date or (b) 90 days from the date of the advance request, based on current SOFR rates and the projected outstanding loan balance. If the test is not satisfied, the Borrower must deposit additional funds into the interest reserve sub-account before the requested advance will be funded.\n\n8\\. **Equity First.** The equity escrow deposit (if required per Condition 10.1.2) must have been fully disbursed and applied to project costs before any loan advance (other than the initial closing advance, Draw 1) is made.\n\n9\\. **Insurance Compliance.** Delivery of evidence of continued compliance with all insurance requirements specified in Section 8.1.\n\n10\\. **No Material Adverse Change.** No material adverse change has occurred in the financial condition of the Borrower, any guarantor, or the Project since the date of closing or the most recent advance, as applicable.\n\n11\\. **Budget Reconciliation.** Delivery of an updated sources and uses reconciliation demonstrating that the Project remains within budget. If cost overruns exist, the Borrower must fund the overruns from equity (not loan proceeds or mezzanine loan proceeds) before any further advances will be made.\n\n**[10.3 Ongoing Covenants and Reporting Requirements]{.underline}**\n\nThe loan documentation will include the following ongoing covenants and reporting requirements, which shall remain in effect throughout the term of the loan (including any extension period):\n\n> • **Minimum Borrower Net Worth:** \\$12,000,000, tested annually within 120 days of the Borrower\\'s fiscal year end, with delivery of a certified net worth certificate.\n>\n> • **Minimum Combined Guarantor Liquidity:** \\$5,000,000, tested quarterly with delivery of guarantor financial statements.\n>\n> • **Negative Pledge / No Additional Debt:** The Borrower may not incur any additional indebtedness, create any additional liens or encumbrances on the Property or any interest therein, or pledge any assets, without the Bank\\'s prior written consent.\n>\n> • **Monthly Construction Reports:** Delivery of monthly construction progress reports to the Bank, including schedule updates, budget reconciliations, change order logs, and third-party inspector certifications.\n>\n> • **SPE Covenants:** The Borrower shall at all times maintain its status as a single-purpose entity in compliance with all SPE covenants set forth in the loan agreement, including maintenance of separate books and records, no commingling of assets or funds, arm\\'s-length transactions with affiliates, independent manager provision, and all other separateness and identity requirements.\n>\n> • **No Material Modifications:** No material modification to the approved construction plans, specifications, or the GMP contract without the Bank\\'s prior written consent. Any modification requiring Brightleaf Historic District Association approval must obtain such approval before the Bank\\'s consent is given.\n>\n> • **Governmental Compliance:** The Borrower shall maintain all governmental permits, licenses, and approvals required for the construction and operation of the Project.\n>\n> • **Environmental Compliance:** The Borrower shall comply with all applicable environmental laws and regulations and with all conditions of the NFA letter dated September 30, 2024, including the installation and ongoing maintenance of the soil vapor mitigation system in the structured parking garage.\n>\n> • **Transfer Restrictions:** No transfer, assignment, or encumbrance of any membership interest in the Borrower without the Bank\\'s prior written consent, subject to the intercreditor agreement provisions regarding the mezzanine lender\\'s subordinated pledge.\n>\n> • **Quarterly Guarantor Reporting:** Delivery of quarterly personal financial statements from each guarantor.\n>\n> • **Prompt Notice:** The Borrower shall provide prompt written notice to the Bank of any litigation, environmental claim, governmental enforcement action, casualty, condemnation proceeding, or other material adverse development affecting the Borrower, any guarantor, or the Project.\n\n**[Section 11: Extension Option Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe executed term sheet provides for one 6-month extension of the mini-perm period, extending the loan maturity from August 28, 2028 to February 28, 2029. The term sheet states that the extension is \\\"subject to conditions\\\" without specifying the particular conditions. The Senior Loan Committee recommends that the following extension conditions be incorporated into the loan documentation:\n\n\\(a\\) **No Default.** No event of default exists and no condition exists that with notice or lapse of time would constitute an event of default under the loan documents or the intercreditor agreement.\n\n\\(b\\) **Completion.** Substantial completion of the Project has been achieved, and a final certificate of occupancy has been issued by the City of Durham for all units and commercial spaces in the Project.\n\n\\(c\\) **Minimum Occupancy.** Not less than 75% of the residential units in the Project are leased to tenants under executed leases with terms of at least 12 months and are physically occupied as of the date of the extension request.\n\n\\(d\\) **Minimum DSCR.** The debt service coverage ratio, calculated based on actual trailing three-month annualized in-place net operating income divided by the annual debt service on the outstanding loan balance at the then-current interest rate, is not less than 1.15x. This threshold is intentionally lower than the 1.25x underwriting standard to account for the expectation that the property may still be in lease-up at the time of the extension.\n\n\\(e\\) **Extension Fee.** Payment of an extension fee equal to 0.25% of the then-outstanding principal balance of the loan.\n\n\\(f\\) **Interest Rate Cap.** Delivery of evidence of an interest rate cap agreement for the extension period, with a cap strike rate approved by the Bank in its reasonable discretion, from a counterparty with a minimum long-term credit rating of A- (S&P) or A3 (Moody\\'s).\n\n\\(g\\) **Intercreditor Agreement.** The intercreditor agreement between the Bank and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC remains in full force and effect, and no default exists thereunder.\n\n\\(h\\) **Updated Appraisal.** Delivery of an updated appraisal (from an MAI-certified appraiser acceptable to the Bank) confirming that the LTV ratio based on the outstanding loan balance and the as-stabilized appraised value does not exceed 70%.\n\n\\(i\\) **Representations and Warranties.** All representations and warranties contained in the loan documents remain true and correct in all material respects.\n\n\\(j\\) **Notice.** Written notice of the Borrower\\'s election to exercise the extension option, delivered to the Bank not less than 60 days prior to the mini-perm maturity date of August 28, 2028.\n\nThese extension conditions are designed to ensure that the Bank\\'s credit position remains satisfactory at the time of the extension and that the Project is on a trajectory toward stabilization and permanent take-out financing.\n\n**[Section 12: Regulatory and Compliance Considerations]{.underline}**\n\nPinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. is a nationally chartered savings bank regulated by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\\\"OCC\\\"). As of the Bank\\'s most recent Call Report, total consolidated assets are approximately \\$18,600,000,000. The proposed loan of \\$47,500,000 represents approximately 0.26% of total bank assets.\n\n**Concentration Risk.** The Bank monitors its commercial real estate lending concentration relative to Tier 1 capital plus the allowance for loan and lease losses (\\\"ALLL\\\"), consistent with the interagency guidance on concentrations of credit risk issued by the OCC, Federal Reserve, and FDIC. The Bank\\'s current CRE concentration levels remain within its board-approved concentration limits, and this loan does not cause any concentration threshold to be exceeded. The Bank\\'s CRE portfolio management team has confirmed that the loan falls within the Bank\\'s approved risk appetite and concentration parameters.\n\n**Lending Policy Compliance.** The proposed loan is consistent with the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy with respect to maximum LTV (70% for construction loans --- the as-completed LTV of 65.25% is within this limit), minimum DSCR (1.25x stabilized --- the projected DSCR of 1.31x is within this limit), and maximum LTC (80% --- the LTC of 83.77% exceeds this limit but will be brought into compliance through the equity escrow condition).\n\n**FIRREA Appraisal Requirements.** The appraisal by Linda M. Haverford, MAI, of Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. meets the requirements of FIRREA and OCC appraisal regulations. The appraiser is MAI-certified, is independent of the transaction parties, and the appraisal is dated November 15, 2024, which is within 12 months of the anticipated closing date.\n\n**Flood Determination.** A flood zone determination will be obtained prior to closing to determine whether any portion of the Property is located within a Special Flood Hazard Area as designated by FEMA. If any portion of the Property is in a flood zone, the Bank will require flood insurance in the amount required by the National Flood Insurance Act and the Bank\\'s policy.\n\n**Bank Secrecy Act / Anti-Money Laundering.** The Bank has completed Know Your Customer (\\\"KYC\\\") and Customer Due Diligence (\\\"CDD\\\") procedures on the Borrower, all members (Elena Vasquez-Torres, Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, Thomas R. Chen, and Reginald K. Osei), and all guarantors. No adverse findings, matches to OFAC lists, or suspicious activity indicators were identified during the KYC/CDD review.\n\n**Fair Lending.** This is a commercial purpose loan to a limited liability company for the construction of a mixed-use development. Fair lending regulations applicable to consumer and residential mortgage lending are not applicable to this transaction.\n\n**HVCRE Classification.** This loan likely qualifies as a High Volatility Commercial Real Estate (\\\"HVCRE\\\") exposure under the Basel III capital rules (12 C.F.R. Part 3, Subpart D for national banks). HVCRE classification applies to acquisition, development, and construction loans where the borrower\\'s contributed capital is less than 15% of the real estate\\'s appraised as-completed value. The Borrower\\'s contributed capital, including the proposed equity escrow of \\$2,140,000 and the land at acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000, totals \\$10,340,000. As a percentage of the as-completed appraised value of \\$72,800,000, the contributed capital equals \\$10,340,000 ÷ \\$72,800,000 = **14.2%**, which is below the 15% threshold. To avoid HVCRE classification, total contributed capital would need to be at least \\$72,800,000 × 15% = \\$10,920,000, requiring an additional \\$580,000 beyond the proposed equity escrow of \\$2,140,000. The Senior Loan Committee accepts the HVCRE classification for this loan and acknowledges the resulting 150% risk weight for regulatory capital purposes.\n\n**[Section 13: Third-Party Reports and Due Diligence Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe Bank has obtained or reviewed the following third-party reports and due diligence materials in connection with its underwriting of this loan:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Report / Item**                **Provider**                                                                      **Date**            **Status**\n  -------------------------------- --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- ------------------- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Appraisal                        Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. (Linda M. Haverford, MAI)                       November 15, 2024   Satisfactory\n\n  Phase I ESA                      Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.)                 October 28, 2024    Satisfactory with conditions (see Sections 8.2, 9.5)\n\n  Title Commitment                 Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (Patricia Langley), Commitment No. NC-2024-88712   December 5, 2024    Satisfactory, subject to payoff of prior deed of trust (Triangle Commercial Bank, \\$3,400,000)\n\n  Zoning                           City of Durham, MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown)                                     Confirmed           Permitted as of right; zoning compliance letter to be obtained prior to closing\n\n  ALTA/NSPS Survey                 To be obtained                                                                    Prior to closing    Pending\n\n  GMP Contract                     Graystone Construction Group Inc. (Mark D. Sullivan, President)                   GMP: \\$34,750,000   To be reviewed by Bank\\'s counsel and construction inspector prior to closing\n\n  Architect\\'s Contract            Meridian Design Collective PC                                                     Current             To be reviewed and assigned to Bank prior to closing\n\n  Organizational Documents         Borrower / Borrower\\'s counsel                                                    Reviewed            Operating agreement requires amendment for SPE covenants and independent manager (see Section 9.3)\n\n  Borrower Financial Statements    Harborview Capital Partners LLC                                                   Current             Reviewed; net worth \\$14,200,000\n\n  Guarantor Financial Statements   Elena Vasquez-Torres; Reginald K. Osei                                            December 2024       Reviewed; combined net worth \\$41,300,000; combined liquidity \\$9,300,000\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll pending items must be completed and delivered to the Bank\\'s satisfaction prior to closing.\n\n**[Section 14: Recommendation and Approval]{.underline}**\n\n**[14.1 Recommendation]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the foregoing analysis, the Commercial Real Estate Lending group recommends **APPROVAL** of the \\$47,500,000 construction-to-mini-permanent loan to Harborview Capital Partners LLC for The Piedmont at Brightleaf project, subject to all conditions set forth in Section 10 and resolution of the risk factors identified in Section 9.\n\nThe following conditions are designated as critical and must be fully satisfied prior to closing:\n\n1\\. **Equity Escrow.** Deposit of \\$2,140,000 in verified cash equity into a Bank-controlled escrow account to bring the effective LTC into compliance with the Bank\\'s 80% maximum (Sections 5.3, 9.1, 10.1.2).\n\n2\\. **Intercreditor Agreement.** Full execution and delivery of the intercreditor agreement with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC resolving the conflicting membership interest pledges and providing for subordination, standstill, cure rights, and bankruptcy protections (Sections 4.4, 9.4, 10.1.3).\n\n3\\. **SPE Covenants and Non-Consolidation Opinion.** Amendment of the Borrower\\'s operating agreement to include SPE covenants and an independent manager provision, and delivery of a non-consolidation opinion from Borrower\\'s counsel (Sections 9.3, 10.1.4).\n\n4\\. **Interest Reserve Replenishment Covenant.** Inclusion of mandatory borrower replenishment obligations and adequacy testing at each draw (Sections 6.2, 9.2, 10.2.7).\n\n5\\. **Environmental Resolution.** Resolution of the NFA use restriction conflict regarding the 14 residential units above the restricted area (Sections 8.2, 9.5, 10.1.7).\n\n6\\. **Title Clearance.** Satisfaction of the prior Triangle Commercial Bank deed of trust and issuance of satisfactory lender\\'s title insurance (Sections 9.7, 10.1.5).\n\nThe loan presents acceptable credit risk given the strong project fundamentals, favorable LTV ratios (65.25% as-completed, 62.34% as-stabilized), the experienced development sponsor, and the adequate combined guarantor financial support (\\$41,300,000 net worth, \\$9,300,000 liquidity). The identified structural issues --- including the LTC exceedance, zero borrower cash equity, interest reserve adequacy, and affiliate/SPE concerns --- are addressable through the enumerated conditions of approval and appropriate loan documentation provisions. The Commercial Real Estate Lending group is confident that these conditions can be satisfied prior to the target closing date of February 28, 2025.\n\n**[14.2 Approval Signatures]{.underline}**\n\n**SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE --- APPROVAL** **Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.** **Loan Number: CRE-2025-0342** **Date: January 22, 2025**\n\n**PREPARED BY / RECOMMENDED:**\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: David Forsythe\n\nTitle: Senior Vice President, Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\nDate: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\n**REVIEWED AND CONCURRED:**\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nTitle: Senior Credit Officer\n\nDate: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\n**APPROVED:**\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nTitle: Chair, Senior Loan Committee\n\nDate: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\n**APPROVED BY SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE --- January 22, 2025**\n\n**Committee Comments / Additional Conditions:**\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n**[Appendix A: Summary of Key Terms (One-Page Summary)]{.underline}**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Term**                            **Detail**\n  ----------------------------------- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Borrower**                        Harborview Capital Partners LLC\n\n  **Guarantors**                      Elena Vasquez-Torres; Reginald K. Osei\n\n  **Loan Amount**                     \\$47,500,000\n\n  **Loan Type**                       Construction-to-Mini-Permanent\n\n  **Construction Period**             30 months (maturity August 28, 2027)\n\n  **Mini-Perm Period**                12 months (maturity August 28, 2028)\n\n  **Extension Option**                One 6-month extension (to February 28, 2029), subject to conditions\n\n  **Rate (Construction)**             Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25%; 1.00% SOFR floor\n\n  **Rate (Mini-Perm)**                Daily Simple SOFR + 3.50%; 1.00% SOFR floor\n\n  **Default Rate**                    Contract rate + 5.00%\n\n  **Payments**                        Interest-only, monthly\n\n  **Origination Fee**                 1.00% (\\$475,000)\n\n  **Exit Fee**                        0.50% (construction); 0.25% (first 6 mos. mini-perm); none thereafter\n\n  **Unused Line Fee**                 0.25% per annum\n\n  **Interest Reserve**                \\$4,850,000\n\n  **Collateral**                      First priority deed of trust, assignment of rents/leases, UCC filings, membership interest pledge (subject to intercreditor), assignment of contracts\n\n  **LTV (As-Completed)**              65.25% (max 70%)\n\n  **LTV (As-Stabilized)**             62.34% (max 70%)\n\n  **LTC**                             83.77% (max 80%) --- requires \\$2,140,000 equity escrow\n\n  **DSCR (Stabilized)**               1.31x (min 1.25x)\n\n  **Mezzanine Loan**                  \\$9,200,000 from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC at 12.50% fixed\n\n  **Total Project Cost**              \\$56,700,000\n\n  **As-Completed Value**              \\$72,800,000\n\n  **As-Stabilized Value**             \\$76,200,000\n\n  **Key Conditions**                  \\(1\\) \\$2,140,000 equity escrow; (2) Intercreditor agreement; (3) SPE covenants / independent manager / non-consolidation opinion; (4) Interest reserve replenishment covenant; (5) Environmental use restriction resolution; (6) Title clearance\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Appendix B: Organizational Chart (Diagram Description)]{.underline}**\n\nThe following describes the organizational and capital structure of the Borrower and the Project:\n\n\\`\\`\\` ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ THOMAS R. CHEN │ │ (Common Control) │ │ ┌──────────┴──────────┐ │ │ ▼ ▼ │ │ Brightleaf Investors Brightleaf Mezzanine │ │ Group LLC Capital LLC │ │ (NC SOS #2045612) (NC SOS #2103988) │ │ 28% Member \\$9,200,000 Mezz Loan │ │ │ (12.50% fixed) │ │ │ Secured by: 100% │ │ │ membership interests │ │ │ (2nd priority per │ │ │ intercreditor) │ │ ▼ │ │ │ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │ │ HARBORVIEW CAPITAL PARTNERS LLC │◄───────────┘ │ │ (Borrower --- NC LLC, SOS #1923847) │ │ │ 400 W. Main St., Suite 310, Durham, NC │ │ │ │ │ │ Members: │ │ │ • Elena Vasquez-Torres --- 62% (Managing Member) │ │ │ • Brightleaf Investors Group LLC --- 28% │ │ │ • Reginald K. Osei --- 10% │ │ └─────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘ │ │ Owns │ ▼ │ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │ THE PIEDMONT AT BRIGHTLEAF │ │ │ 601 & 615 Foster Street, Durham, NC 27701 │ │ │ 212 Units / 18,400 SF Retail / 276 Parking │ │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ ▲ │ │ Senior Loan: \\$47,500,000 │ │ Secured by: 1st priority deed of trust + │ │ 1st priority membership interest pledge │ │ (per intercreditor) │ ┌─────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┐ │ │ PINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A. │ │ │ (Senior Lender) │ │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nGUARANTORS (Completion, Carry, 25% Partial Repayment): • Elena Vasquez-Torres (Net Worth: \\$29,800,000) • Reginald K. Osei (Net Worth: \\$11,500,000) \\`\\`\\`\n\n**[Appendix C: Sources and Uses Table (Full Detail)]{.underline}**\n\n**[Sources of Funds]{.underline}**\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Source**                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Senior Construction Loan (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.)   \\$47,500,000            83.77%\n\n  Mezzanine Loan (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC)        \\$9,200,000             16.23%\n\n  Borrower Cash Equity                                     \\$0                     0.00%\n\n  **Total Sources**                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Uses of Funds]{.underline}**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Use**                                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Land Acquisition (previously closed)                                  \\$8,200,000             14.46%\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP --- Graystone Construction Group Inc.)   \\$34,750,000            61.29%\n\n  Soft Costs (architecture, engineering, permits, legal, accounting)    \\$4,150,000             7.32%\n\n  Interest Reserve                                                      \\$4,850,000             8.55%\n\n  Loan Origination Fee (1.00% × \\$47,500,000)                           \\$475,000               0.84%\n\n  Developer Fee (5.0% of total project cost)                            \\$2,835,000             5.00%\n\n  Contingency                                                           \\$1,440,000             2.54%\n\n  **Total Uses**                                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Reconciliation:** Total Sources (\\$56,700,000) = Total Uses (\\$56,700,000).\n\nEND OF CREDIT MEMORANDUM --- CRE-2025-0342 --- Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. --- CONFIDENTIAL\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_ytOTUqMBc1nJDwqin6zd6645", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/credit-memorandum.docx\", \"offset\": 560}"}
