{"turn": 8, "role": "tool", "result": "**[PHASE I ENVIRONMENTAL SITE ASSESSMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**[EXCERPTED SECTIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[(Findings, Conclusions, and Recommendations)]{.underline}**\n\n**THE PIEDMONT AT BRIGHTLEAF**\n\n**601 and 615 Foster Street** **Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701**\n\n**Parcel IDs: 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102**\n\n**Prepared for:**\n\n**Harborview Capital Partners LLC** 400 West Main Street, Suite 310 Durham, North Carolina 27701\n\nand\n\n**Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.** (as Intended Reliance Party / Lender) 200 South Tryon Street, 18th Floor Charlotte, North Carolina 28202\n\n**Prepared by:**\n\n**Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC** 2700 Battleground Avenue, Suite 400 Greensboro, North Carolina 27408\n\n**Lead Consultant:** Dr. James Whitfield, P.E. Senior Principal Environmental Scientist\n\n**Report Date:** October 28, 2024\n\n**Sentinel Project No.: SEC-2024-1147**\n\nThis document contains excerpted sections from the full Phase I Environmental Site Assessment report prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC under Project No. SEC-2024-1147. The complete report, including all appendices, historical research documentation, regulatory database search results, interview records, and photographic documentation, is available under separate cover. This excerpted document is intended to provide a summary of findings, conclusions, and recommendations for the convenience of the client and the intended reliance party. It does not replace or supersede the full report and should be read in conjunction with the complete Phase I Environmental Site Assessment.\n\nThis assessment was conducted in conformance with ASTM Standard Practice E1527-21, *Standard Practice for Environmental Site Assessments: Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Process*, published by ASTM International, and in compliance with the All Appropriate Inquiries (AAI) rule codified at 40 C.F.R. Part 312.\n\nRight-click to update Table of Contents\n\n**[1. Executive Summary of Findings]{.underline}**\n\nSentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (\\\"Sentinel\\\") was retained by Harborview Capital Partners LLC (\\\"Harborview\\\" or \\\"Client\\\") to conduct a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"Phase I ESA\\\" or \\\"Assessment\\\") of the property located at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701 (the \\\"Subject Property\\\"). The assessment was conducted for the purpose of supporting proposed financing by Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (\\\"Pinnacle\\\" or \\\"Lender\\\") and to satisfy the requirements of the All Appropriate Inquiries (\\\"AAI\\\") rule under 40 C.F.R. Part 312, thereby supporting potential assertion of the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (\\\"CERCLA\\\") innocent landowner, contiguous property owner, and bona fide prospective purchaser defenses.\n\nThe Subject Property consists of two contiguous parcels: Parcel ID 0821-04-72-3341 (approximately 1.89 acres, fronting 601 Foster Street) and Parcel ID 0821-04-72-4102 (approximately 1.58 acres, fronting 615 Foster Street), totaling approximately 3.47 acres of land. The Subject Property is situated within Durham\\'s historic Brightleaf tobacco district, an area that has experienced significant redevelopment over the past two decades. The property is currently vacant improved land. Former tobacco warehouse structures that had occupied both parcels were demolished in August 2024, and environmental remediation activities were completed in September 2024. The site is graded and secured with temporary construction fencing.\n\nThe property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown) by the City of Durham and is planned for development as \\\"The Piedmont at Brightleaf,\\\" a 212-unit Class A mixed-use residential and retail development. The proposed project includes 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail space and a 276-space structured parking garage. The building is designed as a 6-story structure --- five residential levels over one podium level --- with a total gross building area of approximately 287,500 square feet. The project architect is Meridian Design Collective PC, and the general contractor is Graystone Construction Group Inc. (NC License No. 78442).\n\nBased on the assessment conducted herein, one Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\") was identified at the Subject Property. The REC consists of residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") located in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The former UST, a 2,000-gallon diesel fuel tank, was historically associated with a backup generator that served the tobacco warehouse operations. The REC has been subject to a Phase II Environmental Site Assessment investigation (August 2024) and subsequent remediation consisting of soil excavation and off-site disposal (September 2024). A No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") determination letter was issued by the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\"), Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch (\\\"IHSB\\\"), on September 30, 2024, for Site ID IHSB-24-1203.\n\nThe NFA letter is conditional. It is premised upon (a) the installation of a soil vapor mitigation system beneath any enclosed structure constructed over the affected area, (b) a restriction of future use to non-residential purposes on the affected approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, (c) compliance with a soil management plan for future excavation activities within the affected area, and (d) the recording of a deed notice in the Durham County Registry of Deeds within 60 days of the NFA letter date.\n\nNo other Recognized Environmental Conditions, Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions (\\\"CRECs\\\"), or Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions (\\\"HRECs\\\") were identified during this assessment. Several de minimis conditions were noted during the site reconnaissance and are described in the full report; however, these conditions do not rise to the level of RECs under ASTM E1527-21.\n\n**[2. Site Description and History]{.underline}**\n\n**2.1 Physical Description**\n\nThe Subject Property is composed of two contiguous parcels at 601 Foster Street (Parcel 0821-04-72-3341, approximately 1.89 acres) and 615 Foster Street (Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, approximately 1.58 acres), located in downtown Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701. The combined area of the two parcels is approximately 3.47 acres. The site fronts Foster Street to the north and is bounded by commercial and mixed-use properties on the remaining sides.\n\nThe topography of the Subject Property is generally flat, with a gentle slope of approximately 2 percent from northwest to southeast. Site elevation ranges from approximately 395 feet to 401 feet above mean sea level, as confirmed by topographic survey and regional United States Geological Survey data. The site drains generally toward the southeast, consistent with the surrounding topographic gradient and the regional drainage pattern toward the Ellerbe Creek watershed.\n\nThe site is currently vacant. All former structures have been demolished and removed as of August 2024. The ground surface has been graded and the site is enclosed by temporary six-foot chain-link construction fencing with green privacy screening. Existing utility infrastructure --- including municipal water and sanitary sewer lines, electrical service, natural gas, and telecommunications conduit --- has been stubbed to the property boundaries in anticipation of the proposed development. A 20-foot utility easement runs along the western boundary of the site and is occupied by Duke Energy utility poles and overhead electrical lines. A stormwater management easement of approximately 0.22 acres, recorded in the Durham County Registry of Deeds at Book 7421, Page 212, occupies the southern portion of the site and contains a graded drainage swale and inlet structure.\n\n**2.2 Historical Use Summary**\n\nThe historical use of the Subject Property was researched through review of Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps (1901, 1913, 1924, 1937, 1950), historical aerial photographs (1955, 1965, 1972, 1981, 1993, 2005, 2012, 2019), city directories (various years, 1910--2005), Durham County building permit records, Durham County tax records, and interviews with persons knowledgeable about the property. The following chronological summary was developed from these sources:\n\n**1900--1965:** Both parcels were occupied by tobacco warehouses and associated processing buildings. The site was part of Durham\\'s historic Brightleaf tobacco district, which was the center of the American bright-leaf tobacco industry during the early twentieth century. Sanborn maps from 1901 through 1950 depict large, multi-bay warehouse structures on both parcels, with rail siding access along the southern boundary of the property. The warehouses were used for the curing, grading, and storage of leaf tobacco, and later for the manufacture of tobacco products. These operations were consistent with the industrial character of the surrounding area during this period.\n\n**1965--1990:** Following the decline of the Durham tobacco industry, the warehouse buildings were converted to light industrial and general commercial storage uses. City directory listings from this period indicate tenants including a furniture refinishing operation, a printing company, and a general storage and distribution business. During this period, a 2,000-gallon underground storage tank was installed on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to store diesel fuel for a backup generator serving the warehouse operations. Based on a review of Durham County building permit records, the estimated installation date of the UST is approximately 1972. The UST was reportedly decommissioned and removed in approximately 1998; however, no formal UST closure records were located in the files of the Durham County Fire Marshal or the NCDEQ UST Section despite Sentinel\\'s diligent efforts to obtain such records.\n\n**1990--2005:** Light industrial use continued at diminishing levels. Several tenants vacated the property during the 1990s, and by the early 2000s the warehouse buildings were largely unoccupied. Aerial photographs from 1993 and 2005 show the buildings in increasingly deteriorated condition, with portions of the roofing compromised and vegetation encroaching on the southern portions of the site. Surface parking occupied portions of the unpaved areas between the buildings during this period.\n\n**2005--2022:** The property was largely vacant, with the warehouse structures remaining in place but unoccupied. The site was used intermittently as unpaved surface parking, primarily serving visitors to the adjacent Brightleaf Historic District and American Tobacco Campus. Ownership of the parcels changed multiple times during this period, as reflected in the Durham County deed records.\n\n**2022--Present:** Harborview Capital Partners LLC acquired the Subject Property in 2022 for the purpose of redevelopment. A pre-demolition hazardous materials survey was conducted by an independent consultant in July 2024, and demolition of the remaining warehouse structures was completed in August 2024. Environmental remediation, consisting of excavation and off-site disposal of petroleum-impacted soil in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, was completed in September 2024.\n\n**2.3 Surrounding Land Uses and Environmental Setting**\n\nThe Subject Property is situated in a mixed commercial and entertainment district in downtown Durham. Surrounding land uses include the Brightleaf Historic District (commercial retail and restaurant uses) to the north and west, the American Tobacco Campus (mixed-use commercial, office, and entertainment development) to the south, and the Durham Performing Arts Center and associated commercial development to the east. These surrounding properties are generally developed with commercial or institutional uses and do not present obvious indicators of significant environmental concern with respect to the Subject Property.\n\nThe site is located in the Durham Triassic Basin, a geological formation characterized by interbedded sandstone, siltstone, and mudstone of the Newark Supergroup. Depth to groundwater in the vicinity of the Subject Property is estimated at approximately 15 to 25 feet below grade, based on regional hydrogeological data and measurements obtained from monitoring wells installed during the Phase II investigation. The inferred groundwater flow direction is generally to the southeast, consistent with the regional topographic gradient.\n\nThe Subject Property is not located within a Federal Emergency Management Agency (\\\"FEMA\\\") designated Special Flood Hazard Area. According to FEMA Flood Insurance Rate Map Panel 3720098700J, effective March 2, 2009, the site is located in Zone X, an area of minimal flood hazard.\n\n**[3. Environmental Database and Regulatory Records Review]{.underline}**\n\nA standard ASTM-required environmental database search was conducted by Environmental Data Resources, Inc. (\\\"EDR\\\") for the Subject Property and surrounding areas within the applicable ASTM search radii. The database search date was October 10, 2024, and the complete search report is attached as Appendix A to the full Phase I ESA report.\n\n**3.1 Subject Property Listings**\n\nThe Subject Property was identified in the following regulatory databases:\n\n**NCDEQ Incident Management Database (IMD):** The property at 615 Foster Street (Parcel 0821-04-72-4102) is listed as Site No. NONUST-24-0847 in the NCDEQ Incident Management Database. This listing relates to the petroleum contamination identified during the Phase II investigation conducted in August 2024 in connection with the former 2,000-gallon diesel UST. The incident was reported to NCDEQ on August 12, 2024, following the confirmation of contamination above applicable soil remediation goals.\n\n**NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch Database:** The Subject Property is listed as Site ID IHSB-24-1203 in the NCDEQ IHSB database. The current status of this listing is \\\"NFA --- Conditional\\\" as of September 30, 2024, reflecting the issuance of the conditional No Further Action determination letter described in Section 7 of this report.\n\nThe Subject Property was not identified on the National Priorities List (\\\"NPL\\\"), the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Information System (\\\"CERCLIS\\\"), the Resource Conservation and Recovery Information System (\\\"RCRIS\\\") as either a large-quantity or small-quantity generator of hazardous waste, the North Carolina Pre-Regulatory Landfill Inventory, the North Carolina equivalent superfund list, or any other federal or state database that would indicate the presence of additional environmental conditions beyond those already identified herein.\n\n**3.2 Adjoining and Nearby Property Listings**\n\nThe EDR database search identified several properties within the applicable ASTM search radii that are listed on various environmental databases. Specifically, three nearby properties are listed as active Leaking Underground Storage Tank (\\\"LUST\\\") sites within the one-half mile ASTM search radius. Upon review, these LUST sites are located either cross-gradient or down-gradient of the Subject Property relative to the inferred groundwater flow direction. Based on the distance from the Subject Property, the reported nature and extent of contamination at these off-site locations, and the groundwater flow direction, Sentinel concluded that these nearby LUST sites do not represent RECs for the Subject Property and would not be expected to have impacted the Subject Property\\'s subsurface conditions.\n\nNo other nearby or adjoining property listings were identified that would be expected to have adversely affected the environmental conditions at the Subject Property.\n\n**3.3 UST Records**\n\nNo active underground storage tanks are registered for the Subject Property in the NCDEQ UST database. Historical records, including building permit files and Sanborn map annotations, indicate that a 2,000-gallon diesel UST was present on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 from approximately 1972 to approximately 1998. Despite Sentinel\\'s attempts to obtain formal UST closure documentation from both the Durham County Fire Marshal\\'s office and the NCDEQ UST Section, no closure records were located. The absence of closure records is noted as a data gap, although the Phase II investigation and subsequent remediation have substantively addressed the environmental conditions associated with the former UST.\n\n**3.4 NCDEQ File Review**\n\nSentinel reviewed the NCDEQ project files maintained at the NCDEQ Mooresville Regional Office pertaining to Site No. NONUST-24-0847 and Site ID IHSB-24-1203. The file contains the Phase II investigation report dated August 30, 2024, the remediation work plan and completion report dated September 25, 2024, post-remediation groundwater monitoring data, and the No Further Action letter dated September 30, 2024. The contents of these files are consistent with the information summarized in this report and in the full Phase I ESA.\n\n**[4. Site Reconnaissance Observations]{.underline}**\n\nA site reconnaissance was conducted on October 15, 2024, by Dr. James Whitfield, P.E., Senior Principal Environmental Scientist, and Sarah Ng, Environmental Scientist, both of Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC. The reconnaissance was conducted during daylight hours under clear skies with an ambient temperature of approximately 62°F. Access to the full extent of both parcels was provided by the Client\\'s representative, Mr. Daniel Okafor, Vice President of Development for Harborview Capital Partners LLC.\n\nThe following observations were made during the site reconnaissance:\n\n**General Site Conditions:** The Subject Property was observed to be entirely vacant, with the ground surface graded and stabilized with a combination of compacted soil and imported crushed stone. The entire perimeter of the combined site was enclosed by temporary six-foot chain-link construction fencing with green privacy screening, consistent with site preparation for pending construction activities. No structures of any kind remain on the property. All former tobacco warehouse structures and associated building infrastructure had been demolished and removed prior to the date of the reconnaissance, consistent with the reported August 2024 demolition.\n\n**Northeast Corner --- Remediation Area (Parcel 0821-04-72-4102):** The northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 exhibited areas of recent soil disturbance and backfill, consistent with the documented excavation and remediation activities completed in September 2024. The backfilled area was approximately 100 feet by 165 feet (approximately 0.38 acres) and was distinguishable from the surrounding graded surface by the color and texture of the imported fill material used to backfill the excavation. No staining, odors, or other indicators of residual contamination were observed at the ground surface. Erosion control measures, including silt fencing and a stabilized construction entrance, were in place around the remediation area.\n\n**Remaining Site Areas:** No staining, stressed vegetation, dumped materials, evidence of spills or releases, or other visual indicators of environmental contamination were observed on the remaining portions of the Subject Property. The ground surface in areas outside the remediation zone was uniformly graded and free of debris.\n\n**Fill Material:** A verification stockpile of clean imported fill material (approximately 200 cubic yards of crushed stone and screened topsoil) was observed in the southern portion of the site, staged for use in final grading activities.\n\n**Hazardous Materials:** No polychlorinated biphenyl (PCB)-containing equipment, asbestos-containing materials, or lead-based paint were observed during the reconnaissance. All former structures have been demolished and removed. A pre-demolition hazardous materials survey was conducted by an independent consultant (Southern Environmental Services Inc.) in July 2024; the results of that survey are referenced in the full report and indicated that limited quantities of asbestos-containing materials and lead-based paint were identified and abated prior to demolition. No residual hazardous building materials were observed during Sentinel\\'s reconnaissance.\n\n**Agricultural Use:** No evidence of current or former agricultural use was observed on the Subject Property or identified in the historical research conducted for this assessment.\n\n**Utility Infrastructure:** Capped municipal water and sanitary sewer service lines were observed at the northern and western property boundaries. Electrical conduit and transformer pads were observed along the western boundary within the 20-foot Duke Energy utility easement, which is marked by a series of wooden utility poles carrying overhead distribution lines. Natural gas service was observed stubbed to the northwestern corner of the site. Telecommunications conduit was observed along the northern property boundary. The utility infrastructure appeared to be in good condition and consistent with typical pre-development site preparation.\n\n**Easements and Stormwater Features:** The 20-foot Duke Energy utility easement along the western boundary was observed to be clear of environmental concerns, consisting of a maintained grass strip beneath the overhead utility lines. The stormwater management easement area (approximately 0.22 acres) in the southern portion of the site was observed to contain a graded drainage swale and stormwater inlet structure, consistent with the City of Durham stormwater management requirements and the recorded stormwater easement at Book 7421, Page 212, of the Durham County Registry of Deeds. No standing water or evidence of wetland conditions was observed within the stormwater easement area.\n\n**[5. Phase II Investigation Results (Summary)]{.underline}**\n\n*Note: The Phase II Environmental Site Assessment referenced in this section was performed by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC under a separate scope of work and separate contractual engagement in August 2024. The Phase I ESA does not include sampling or analysis as part of its scope. The following summary is provided for reference and completeness in understanding the environmental conditions identified at the Subject Property. The complete Phase II investigation report is available under separate cover (Sentinel Project No. SEC-2024-1089) and is also referenced in the NCDEQ project files for Site ID IHSB-24-1203.*\n\n**5.1 Scope of Investigation**\n\nThe Phase II investigation was conducted in August 2024 and was focused on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, in the vicinity of the former 2,000-gallon diesel underground storage tank. The investigation scope included the advancement of twelve soil borings (designated SB-01 through SB-12) to depths ranging from 15 to 25 feet below grade, the installation and sampling of three groundwater monitoring wells (designated MW-01, MW-02, and MW-03), and the collection of soil gas samples from eight sub-slab soil gas probes (designated SG-01 through SG-08) installed at a depth of approximately 5 feet below grade. All sampling and analysis were conducted in accordance with NCDEQ-approved sampling protocols, and all samples were analyzed by a North Carolina-certified analytical laboratory (Pace Analytical Services, Huntersville, North Carolina).\n\n**5.2 Soil Analytical Results**\n\nTotal petroleum hydrocarbons in the diesel range organics (\\\"TPH-DRO\\\") fraction were detected in soil samples collected from borings within and adjacent to the former UST location at concentrations ranging from non-detect (below the laboratory method detection limit of 5.0 mg/kg) to a maximum of 847 mg/kg in soil boring SB-07 at a depth of 10 feet below grade. Benzene was detected in soil samples at a maximum concentration of 0.42 mg/kg, also in boring SB-07 at a depth of 8 feet below grade. Additional petroleum-related constituents, including toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes, were detected at lower concentrations in several borings within the affected area.\n\nThe lateral extent of petroleum contamination was generally limited to an area of approximately 0.38 acres in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. Borings SB-05 through SB-09, located within this area, exhibited the highest concentrations, while borings SB-01 through SB-04, SB-10, SB-11, and SB-12, located outside or at the periphery of the affected area, exhibited non-detect or trace-level concentrations. Vertically, the contamination extended from approximately 4 feet to 14 feet below grade, with the highest concentrations observed at depths of 8 to 12 feet, consistent with the inferred depth of the former UST and the surrounding backfill soils.\n\nThe detected concentrations were compared to the NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch Preliminary Soil Remediation Goals (\\\"PSRGs\\\") for both residential and non-residential land uses. The following table summarizes the critical comparisons:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Constituent**   **Maximum Detected Concentration**   **NCDEQ Residential PSRG**   **Exceeds Residential?**   **NCDEQ Non-Residential PSRG**   **Exceeds Non-Residential?**\n  ----------------- ------------------------------------ ---------------------------- -------------------------- -------------------------------- ------------------------------\n  TPH-DRO           847 mg/kg                            100 mg/kg                    Yes                        1,000 mg/kg                      No\n\n  Benzene           0.42 mg/kg                           0.065 mg/kg                  Yes                        0.89 mg/kg                       No\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAs indicated in the table above, the maximum detected concentrations of both TPH-DRO and benzene in soil exceeded the NCDEQ residential PSRGs but were below the NCDEQ non-residential (commercial/industrial) PSRGs. This distinction is significant because the NCDEQ applies different risk-based cleanup standards depending on the intended land use. The residential PSRGs are more stringent because they are designed to be protective of long-term residential exposure, including exposure to young children and other sensitive populations, under a 30-year residential exposure scenario.\n\n**5.3 Groundwater Analytical Results**\n\nDissolved-phase petroleum constituents were detected in groundwater samples collected from monitoring well MW-02, which is located within the affected area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. Specifically, dissolved benzene was detected at a concentration of 0.8 µg/L (the North Carolina 2L Groundwater Quality Standard for benzene is 1.0 µg/L) and dissolved TPH-DRO was detected at 320 µg/L. All other dissolved-phase constituents were either non-detect or detected at concentrations well below applicable 2L Standards. Monitoring wells MW-01 and MW-03, located on the periphery of the affected area, did not exhibit detectable concentrations of dissolved petroleum constituents, indicating that the dissolved-phase groundwater plume has not migrated significantly beyond the immediate vicinity of the former UST location and does not appear to have migrated off-site.\n\n**5.4 Soil Gas Analytical Results**\n\nVolatile organic compounds, including benzene and naphthalene, were detected in soil gas samples collected from probes within and near the affected area. The most significant soil gas detection was benzene at a concentration of 185 µg/m³ in soil gas sample SG-04, which is located within the center of the affected area. The NCDEQ residential vapor intrusion screening level for benzene in sub-slab soil gas is 31 µg/m³. The detected concentration of 185 µg/m³ exceeds this screening level by a factor of approximately six, indicating the potential for vapor intrusion into enclosed structures constructed over the affected area. Naphthalene was also detected in soil gas samples SG-03, SG-04, and SG-05 at concentrations ranging from 22 to 89 µg/m³.\n\nThese soil gas results support the conclusion that a soil vapor mitigation system will be required beneath any enclosed structure constructed over the affected area to prevent the migration of volatile petroleum constituents into indoor air at concentrations that could pose a risk to building occupants.\n\n**[6. Remediation Summary]{.underline}**\n\nFollowing the completion of the Phase II investigation and confirmation that soil contamination in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 exceeded NCDEQ residential PSRGs, Harborview Capital Partners LLC retained Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC to conduct remediation of the identified petroleum contamination. Remediation activities were performed in September 2024 in accordance with a remediation work plan approved by the NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch.\n\n**6.1 Scope of Remediation**\n\nThe primary remediation activity consisted of the excavation and off-site disposal of petroleum-impacted soil from the affected area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. Approximately 1,200 cubic yards of soil were excavated from the affected area, with excavation depths reaching approximately 12 feet below grade in the most heavily impacted zone (corresponding to the area surrounding soil borings SB-06 and SB-07). The excavation was conducted using conventional earthmoving equipment under the oversight of Sentinel field personnel, with continuous air monitoring conducted at the excavation perimeter to ensure worker and community safety.\n\nConfirmation soil samples were collected from the excavation sidewalls and base at the completion of excavation activities. The confirmation sampling results demonstrated that residual contamination remains in situ at concentrations below the NCDEQ non-residential PSRGs throughout the excavation area. However, residual contamination remains above the NCDEQ residential PSRGs in certain areas at depths of 10 to 14 feet below grade, particularly along the eastern property boundary where excavation was limited by the proximity of the adjoining property and critical underground utility infrastructure (a 12-inch sanitary sewer main and associated service laterals). Complete removal of all contamination exceeding residential standards was not feasible without risk of damage to the adjacent underground utilities and potential encroachment onto the adjoining property.\n\nAll impacted soil removed during the excavation was transported under proper chain-of-custody documentation and disposed of at the Lee County Environmental Landfill, a permitted Subtitle D solid waste management facility located in Sanford, North Carolina. Waste characterization sampling confirmed that the impacted soil met the acceptance criteria for Subtitle D disposal and did not exhibit hazardous waste characteristics under 40 C.F.R. Part 261.\n\nGroundwater encountered during excavation was managed in accordance with an NCDEQ-approved water management plan. Approximately 45,000 gallons of groundwater were pumped from the excavation, treated through granular activated carbon filtration, and discharged under a temporary NPDES general permit.\n\n**6.2 Post-Remediation Monitoring**\n\nTwo rounds of post-remediation groundwater monitoring were conducted, on September 15 and October 1, 2024, respectively. Groundwater samples collected from monitoring wells MW-01, MW-02, and MW-03 exhibited decreasing concentrations of dissolved petroleum constituents. All dissolved-phase constituents were detected at concentrations below the applicable North Carolina 2L Groundwater Quality Standards in both monitoring rounds, indicating that the excavation remediation has effectively reduced the dissolved-phase groundwater contamination to below regulatory standards.\n\n**6.3 Remediation Cost**\n\nThe total cost of the remediation activities, including excavation, soil disposal, water management, confirmation sampling, and post-remediation monitoring, was approximately \\$485,000. This cost was funded by Harborview Capital Partners LLC.\n\n**[7. NCDEQ No Further Action (NFA) Letter --- Summary of Conditions]{.underline}**\n\nOn September 30, 2024, the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality, Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch, issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") determination letter for Site ID IHSB-24-1203 / Incident No. NONUST-24-0847 (the \\\"NFA Letter\\\"). The NFA Letter was addressed to Harborview Capital Partners LLC as the current property owner and responsible party. A complete copy of the NFA Letter is attached as Appendix E to the full Phase I ESA report. The following is a summary of the material terms and conditions of the NFA Letter.\n\nThe NFA Letter states that, based on a review of the Phase II investigation data, the remediation completion report, and the post-remediation groundwater monitoring results, NCDEQ has determined that no further remediation action is required at the Subject Property at this time. This determination is expressly conditioned upon compliance with the following requirements:\n\n**Condition 1 --- Soil Vapor Mitigation System.** A soil vapor mitigation system must be installed beneath any enclosed structure constructed over the affected area, which is identified as the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, as delineated in the Phase II investigation report and shown on Figure 3 of this report. The NFA Letter specifies that the system shall consist of a passive sub-slab depressurization system with the capability for conversion to an active depressurization system if subsequent monitoring indicates that passive ventilation is insufficient to maintain indoor air quality below applicable screening levels. The system must be designed by a licensed professional engineer registered in the State of North Carolina and must be installed in accordance with NCDEQ Division of Waste Management guidelines for vapor intrusion mitigation. As-built drawings and a system performance verification report must be submitted to NCDEQ within 90 days of the completion of the building foundation in the affected area.\n\n**Condition 2 --- Land Use Restriction (Non-Residential).** The NFA determination is issued on the basis that the affected portion of the Subject Property --- the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 --- will be restricted to non-residential use. The NFA Letter states, in relevant part:\n\n> \\\"Residual soil contamination concentrations in the affected area exceed the NCDEQ Preliminary Soil Remediation Goals for residential land use. Accordingly, this No Further Action determination is contingent upon the restriction of future use of the affected area to non-residential (commercial or industrial) purposes. Any change in land use to residential or other sensitive use on the affected portion of the parcel would require additional investigation and/or remediation to demonstrate that such use is protective of human health and the environment, and a modification to this NFA determination would need to be obtained from NCDEQ prior to such change in use.\\\"\n\nThis non-residential use restriction is the central condition of the NFA Letter and has direct implications for the proposed development of the Subject Property, as discussed further in Sections 8 and 10 of this report.\n\n**Condition 3 --- Soil Management Plan.** Any future excavation, grading, or soil disturbance within the affected area must be conducted in accordance with a NCDEQ-approved soil management plan to ensure proper identification, handling, and disposal of residual contaminated soil. The soil management plan must address procedures for soil screening, stockpile management, dust control, worker health and safety, and disposal of any excavated soil that exceeds applicable waste characterization thresholds. The NFA Letter requires that the soil management plan be submitted to and approved by NCDEQ prior to commencement of any construction activities involving subsurface disturbance within the affected area.\n\n**Condition 4 --- Deed Notice.** The property owner is required to record a notice in the Durham County Registry of Deeds providing notice to future purchasers, lessees, and other parties of the residual contamination present within the affected area and the land use restriction applicable to the affected area. The NFA Letter requires this deed notice to be recorded within 60 days of the date of the NFA Letter --- that is, by November 29, 2024. The deed notice must include a description of the affected area by metes and bounds or by reference to a recorded plat, a description of the nature and extent of residual contamination, and a statement of the non-residential use restriction.\n\nAs of the date of this Phase I ESA (October 28, 2024), the consultant was not provided evidence that the required deed notice has been recorded. The 60-day recording deadline of November 29, 2024, had not yet passed as of the assessment date. The client should ensure timely recording of the deed notice to maintain compliance with the NFA conditions.\n\nIt is important to note that the NFA Letter does not constitute a \\\"clean closure\\\" or a determination that the Subject Property is free of all environmental contamination. The NFA Letter expressly acknowledges that residual petroleum contamination remains in the subsurface within the affected area at concentrations exceeding residential PSRGs. The NFA determination is site-specific and applies only to the conditions known at the time of its issuance. Changes in site conditions, regulatory standards, or land use that are inconsistent with the conditions of the NFA Letter could require reopening the regulatory file and additional investigation or remediation.\n\n**[8. Findings and Identified Environmental Conditions]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the research, investigation, site reconnaissance, and analysis conducted as part of this Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, Sentinel has identified the following environmental conditions at the Subject Property in accordance with the classification framework established by ASTM Standard Practice E1527-21.\n\n**8.1 Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs)**\n\n**One REC was identified at the Subject Property:**\n\nResidual petroleum contamination, consisting of total petroleum hydrocarbons --- diesel range organics (\\\"TPH-DRO\\\") and benzene, is present in soil and soil gas in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, in the area of a former 2,000-gallon diesel underground storage tank. The contamination affects an area of approximately 0.38 acres. Although remediation has been conducted and a conditional NFA letter has been issued by NCDEQ, the NFA is premised upon ongoing compliance with specified conditions, and residual contamination remains in place at concentrations exceeding the NCDEQ residential Preliminary Soil Remediation Goals for both TPH-DRO and benzene in soil. Volatile organic compounds in soil gas within the affected area also exceed NCDEQ residential vapor intrusion screening levels.\n\nThis condition constitutes a REC as defined in ASTM E1527-21, Section 3.2.84, because the presence of residual petroleum contamination above unrestricted use standards represents \\\"the presence or likely presence of any hazardous substances or petroleum products in, on, or at a property: (i) due to a release to the environment; (ii) under conditions indicative of a release to the environment; or (iii) under conditions that pose a material threat of a future release to the environment.\\\"\n\n*Note regarding classification as REC versus CREC:* The consultant considered whether this condition should be classified as a Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"CREC\\\") rather than a REC. Under ASTM E1527-21, Section 3.2.25, a CREC is defined as \\\"a recognized environmental condition resulting from a past release of hazardous substances or petroleum products that has been addressed to the satisfaction of the applicable regulatory authority...with hazardous substances or petroleum products allowed to remain in place subject to the implementation of required controls.\\\" Given that NCDEQ has issued an NFA determination with conditions --- including the soil vapor mitigation requirement, land use restriction, soil management plan, and deed notice --- this condition possesses many characteristics of a CREC. However, as of the date of this assessment, the required deed notice had not yet been recorded in the Durham County Registry of Deeds, and the required soil vapor mitigation system had not yet been designed or installed. Because the NFA conditions have not been fully implemented, the consultant has classified this condition as a REC, representing a conservative and protective approach. Upon full implementation of all NFA conditions --- including recording of the deed notice, installation and verification of the vapor mitigation system, and submittal of the soil management plan --- this condition may be reclassified as a CREC in a future assessment update.\n\n**8.2 Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions (CRECs)**\n\nNo Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions were identified at the Subject Property as of the date of this assessment. As noted above, the identified REC may be reclassified as a CREC in the future upon full implementation of all NCDEQ NFA conditions.\n\n**8.3 Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions (HRECs)**\n\nNo Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions were identified at the Subject Property.\n\n**8.4 De Minimis Conditions**\n\nThe following de minimis conditions were observed during the site reconnaissance and/or identified through the research conducted for this assessment. These conditions do not rise to the level of RECs under ASTM E1527-21 and are not considered to represent a material threat to human health or the environment:\n\n> • Small areas of fill material of unknown origin were observed at the ground surface in the central portion of the site. The fill material appeared to consist of construction and demolition debris (brick fragments, concrete pieces) mixed with soil, likely associated with the recent demolition of the former warehouse structures. This material did not exhibit staining, odors, or other indicators of contamination.\n>\n> • Incidental surface staining was observed in the former loading dock area on the southwestern portion of Parcel 0821-04-72-3341. The staining was limited in extent (approximately 50 square feet) and appeared consistent with minor historic oil drips from vehicles or equipment. No odors or soil discoloration at depth were observed.\n\nThese de minimis conditions are described in greater detail in the full Phase I ESA report.\n\n**8.5 Business Environmental Risks**\n\nThe conditional NFA land use restriction limiting the affected 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to non-residential use may constitute a business environmental risk to the Client and its lender if the proposed development includes residential use of the affected area. Under ASTM E1527-21, business environmental risks are not required to be identified as part of the standard Phase I ESA scope; however, the consultant notes this potential risk as a matter of professional judgment and in the interest of the Client.\n\nA review of the preliminary site plans provided by the Client (drawing set dated September 12, 2024, prepared by Meridian Design Collective PC) indicates that the proposed development includes a 6-story mixed-use building with a ground-level podium (parking garage and retail) and five levels of residential units on floors 2 through 6. The building footprint extends across substantial portions of both parcels. The consultant did not independently verify whether any residential units would be positioned directly above the affected 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102, as this determination requires a detailed overlay of the proposed building floor plans with the environmental delineation map prepared as part of the Phase II investigation (see Figure 3 --- Site Map, Section 12 of this report). The Client and its design team should verify the spatial relationship between the affected area and any proposed residential occupancy spaces, and should consult with environmental counsel regarding the implications of the NFA land use restriction for the proposed development program.\n\n**[9. Conclusions]{.underline}**\n\nSentinel Environmental Consulting LLC has performed this Phase I Environmental Site Assessment in conformance with the scope and limitations of ASTM Standard Practice E1527-21, *Standard Practice for Environmental Site Assessments: Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Process*. The assessment was conducted for the benefit of Harborview Capital Partners LLC and Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (as intended reliance party / lender) in connection with the proposed development and financing of The Piedmont at Brightleaf at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701.\n\nBased on the findings of this assessment, one Recognized Environmental Condition was identified at the Subject Property: residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The identified REC has been investigated through a Phase II Environmental Site Assessment (August 2024), remediated through soil excavation and off-site disposal (September 2024), and has received a conditional No Further Action determination from the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality, Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch, dated September 30, 2024.\n\nThe conditional NFA determination requires: (1) installation of a soil vapor mitigation system beneath any enclosed structure constructed over the affected area (approximately 0.38 acres), (2) restriction of future use of the affected area to non-residential purposes, (3) adherence to a NCDEQ-approved soil management plan for any future excavation within the affected area, and (4) recording of a deed notice in the Durham County Registry of Deeds within 60 days of the NFA letter date. As of the date of this assessment, the deed notice had not yet been recorded and the soil vapor mitigation system had not yet been installed, although the respective deadlines and design timelines had not yet elapsed.\n\nNo other Recognized Environmental Conditions, Controlled Recognized Environmental Conditions, or Historical Recognized Environmental Conditions were identified during this assessment. Several de minimis conditions were noted but do not constitute RECs.\n\nThis Phase I ESA does not constitute a guarantee or warranty that the Subject Property is free of environmental contamination. The assessment is limited by the scope of ASTM E1527-21 and the information reasonably ascertainable at the time of the assessment, and is further subject to the inherent limitations of any environmental investigation, including the possibility that conditions may exist that were not identified through the assessment methodologies employed. Additional investigation may be warranted for specific concerns outside the scope of this assessment.\n\nThis assessment satisfies the All Appropriate Inquiries requirements of 40 C.F.R. Part 312 as of the date of this report (October 28, 2024). Pursuant to 40 C.F.R. § 312.20(c), the shelf life of this assessment is 180 days from the date of the report --- that is, through April 26, 2025 --- for purposes of AAI compliance, subject to the update requirements set forth in 40 C.F.R. § 312.20(c)(2). If the anticipated loan closing (currently targeted for February 28, 2025) occurs within this 180-day window, no update to this assessment should be required solely for AAI purposes, absent material changes in site conditions or new information.\n\n**[10. Recommendations]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the findings, identified environmental conditions, and conclusions set forth in this Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC offers the following recommendations to the Client and the Lender.\n\n**Recommendation 1 --- Verify Spatial Relationship of Residential Units to Restricted Area.** The Client and its project architect (Meridian Design Collective PC) should verify whether any proposed residential units in The Piedmont at Brightleaf development will be positioned directly above the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 that is subject to the NCDEQ non-residential use restriction under the NFA Letter. This verification should be accomplished by overlaying the proposed building floor plans (including unit layouts for floors 2 through 6) with the environmental delineation map prepared as part of the Phase II investigation (reproduced as Figure 3 in this report). If the overlay confirms that residential units are planned directly above the affected area, the Client should consult with qualified environmental legal counsel regarding the need to either (a) obtain a formal modification to the NFA determination from NCDEQ permitting residential use of the affected area, which would likely require additional environmental investigation, risk assessment, and potentially additional remediation to demonstrate that residential use is protective of human health and the environment under a 30-year residential exposure scenario, or (b) redesign the project to eliminate residential uses above the affected area, such as by repositioning the residential unit layout or converting the affected portion of the upper floors to non-residential uses (e.g., common areas, amenity spaces, or commercial uses). The cost and timeline implications of either approach should be carefully evaluated by the Client, its design team, and its lender.\n\n**Recommendation 2 --- Soil Vapor Mitigation System Design and Installation.** The Client should engage a licensed professional engineer to design the required soil vapor mitigation system for any enclosed space --- including the structured parking garage and any ground-floor retail space --- constructed over the affected area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The system should be designed as a passive sub-slab depressurization system with conversion capability to active depressurization, as specified in the NFA Letter. Design of the vapor mitigation system should commence during the construction design development phase to ensure that the system is fully integrated into the building foundation and structural plans. As-built drawings and a performance verification report must be submitted to NCDEQ within 90 days of the completion of the building foundation in the affected area, as required by the NFA Letter.\n\n**Recommendation 3 --- Record Deed Notice.** The NFA Letter requires the recording of a deed notice in the Durham County Registry of Deeds within 60 days of September 30, 2024 --- that is, by November 29, 2024. The deed notice must describe the affected area, the nature of the residual contamination, and the non-residential use restriction. The Client should confirm that this deed notice has been or will be timely prepared, reviewed by legal counsel, and recorded. Failure to record the deed notice within the required timeframe could jeopardize the NFA status and may constitute non-compliance with the conditions of the NFA determination. The Client\\'s environmental counsel should also review the form and content of the deed notice to ensure that it is consistent with the NFA Letter requirements and does not inadvertently create title encumbrances or restrictions beyond those required by NCDEQ.\n\n**Recommendation 4 --- Soil Management Plan.** A soil management plan should be prepared and submitted to NCDEQ for review and approval prior to commencement of any excavation, grading, or other subsurface disturbance activities within the affected area. The soil management plan should address procedures for visual and olfactory screening of excavated soils, stockpile management, dust suppression, personal protective equipment requirements, worker health and safety protocols, and disposal of any excavated soil that exceeds waste characterization thresholds. All construction personnel working within or adjacent to the affected area should receive training on the potential for encountering contaminated soil and the procedures established in the soil management plan. The soil management plan should be incorporated into the construction specifications and made available to the general contractor (Graystone Construction Group Inc.) and all relevant subcontractors.\n\n**Recommendation 5 --- Ongoing Indoor Air Quality and Soil Gas Monitoring.** The Client should consider voluntarily conducting periodic indoor air quality monitoring or sub-slab soil gas monitoring after construction of any enclosed spaces over the affected area to verify the continued effectiveness of the soil vapor mitigation system. While such monitoring is not expressly required by the NFA Letter, it would provide ongoing documentation that indoor air quality is maintained below applicable screening levels and would serve as an additional safeguard for building occupants. Monitoring should be conducted at a frequency and according to a protocol approved by a qualified environmental professional.\n\n**Recommendation 6 --- Environmental Insurance.** The Client and the Lender (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.) may wish to evaluate the merits of obtaining a pollution legal liability (\\\"PLL\\\") insurance policy to address potential environmental liability associated with the residual contamination at the Subject Property. A PLL policy could provide coverage for costs associated with, among other things, obtaining an NFA modification from NCDEQ, conducting additional remediation if required, defending against third-party claims related to the contamination, and responding to unknown environmental conditions that may be discovered during construction. The availability, terms, and cost of PLL coverage should be discussed with a qualified environmental insurance broker.\n\n**Recommendation 7 --- Update Assessment Before Closing if Necessary.** If the loan closing for the proposed development occurs after April 26, 2025 (180 days from the date of this report), this Phase I ESA will need to be updated in accordance with 40 C.F.R. § 312.20(c) to maintain compliance with the All Appropriate Inquiries requirements. The Client\\'s currently anticipated closing date of February 28, 2025, falls within the 180-day AAI compliance window. However, if the closing is delayed beyond April 26, 2025, the Client should contact Sentinel to arrange for an update to this assessment, which would typically include an updated site reconnaissance, a review of any new regulatory actions or database listings, and an updated assessment of environmental conditions. The cost and scope of an update are typically less than a full Phase I ESA but should be budgeted in the event of a closing delay.\n\n**[11. Limitations and Qualifications]{.underline}**\n\nThis Phase I Environmental Site Assessment was conducted in accordance with the scope and limitations of ASTM Standard Practice E1527-21 and the All Appropriate Inquiries rule codified at 40 C.F.R. Part 312. The assessment is subject to the inherent limitations of those standards and the following qualifications:\n\nThis Phase I ESA did not include sampling or analysis of soil, groundwater, soil gas, surface water, air, or building materials. Sampling and analytical activities were conducted under a separate Phase II Environmental Site Assessment scope of work (Sentinel Project No. SEC-2024-1089) and are summarized in Section 5 of this report for reference purposes only. The Phase I ESA scope is limited to non-invasive inquiry as defined by ASTM E1527-21.\n\nThe findings, conclusions, and recommendations set forth in this report are based on information reasonably ascertainable to the consultant as of the date of this assessment (October 28, 2024). Environmental conditions are dynamic, and the findings of this assessment are subject to change based on new information, changes in regulatory standards or agency interpretations, additional site investigation, or the discovery of previously unknown conditions. This report represents a snapshot in time and should not be relied upon as a definitive characterization of all environmental conditions at the Subject Property.\n\nThis Phase I ESA was performed for the exclusive use and benefit of Harborview Capital Partners LLC (as client) and Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (as intended reliance party / lender). No other party may rely on this report or any of its findings without the prior written consent of Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC. Reliance by Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. is governed by and subject to the terms and conditions of the reliance letter dated October 28, 2024, which is attached as Appendix G to the full Phase I ESA report (not included in this excerpted document).\n\nThis report does not constitute a guarantee or warranty of any kind, either express or implied, regarding the environmental condition of the Subject Property. The report should not be construed as legal advice, and the Client and the Lender are advised to consult with qualified environmental legal counsel regarding any regulatory compliance, liability, or transactional issues arising from the environmental conditions identified herein.\n\nSentinel Environmental Consulting LLC\\'s aggregate liability under this engagement is limited to the professional service fee paid for the Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, which was approximately \\$12,500. This limitation of liability is set forth in the professional services agreement between Sentinel and Harborview Capital Partners LLC dated September 20, 2024, and is incorporated herein by reference.\n\n**[12. Certification and Signature]{.underline}**\n\nI declare that, to the best of my professional knowledge and belief, I meet the definition of Environmental Professional as defined in §312.10 of 40 C.F.R. Part 312. I have the specific qualifications based on education, training, and experience to assess a property of the nature, history, and setting of the Subject Property. I have performed this Phase I Environmental Site Assessment in conformance with the scope and limitations of ASTM Practice E1527-21, *Standard Practice for Environmental Site Assessments: Phase I Environmental Site Assessment Process*. This certification is made in accordance with the requirements of ASTM E1527-21, Section 12.\n\nI have no direct or indirect current or prospective personal interest in the Subject Property, and I have no personal bias with respect to the parties involved. The compensation received for this assessment is not contingent upon the findings, conclusions, or recommendations contained herein.\n\nSENTINEL ENVIRONMENTAL CONSULTING LLC\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.\n\nTitle: Senior Principal Environmental Scientist\n\nNC Professional Engineer License No. 038271\n\nDate: October 28, 2024\n\n**Quality Assurance Review:**\n\nSENTINEL ENVIRONMENTAL CONSULTING LLC\n\nReviewed by: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Rachel M. Dobson\n\nTitle: Director of Environmental Assessment Services\n\nDate: October 28, 2024\n\n**[Figure 3 --- Site Map: Identified Environmental Conditions and Affected Area]{.underline}**\n\n**\\[TEXTUAL DESCRIPTION OF FIGURE\\]**\n\n*The following textual description is provided in lieu of a graphic figure for the purposes of this document. The original Figure 3 is a scaled site map prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC and is included in both the full Phase I ESA report and as a standalone figure available upon request.*\n\n**Title:** Figure 3 --- Site Map: Identified Environmental Conditions and Affected Area\n\n**Map Orientation:** North arrow oriented toward the top of the figure. Approximate scale: 1 inch = 60 feet.\n\n**Parcel Layout:** The map depicts the two contiguous parcels comprising the Subject Property. Parcel 0821-04-72-3341 (the western parcel, approximately 1.89 acres, addressed as 601 Foster Street) is shown on the left (western) portion of the map. Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (the eastern parcel, approximately 1.58 acres, addressed as 615 Foster Street) is shown on the right (eastern) portion of the map. The parcel boundary between the two parcels is indicated by a dashed line running approximately north-south through the center of the combined site. Foster Street is depicted along the northern boundary of the combined site.\n\n**Affected Area:** The affected area of approximately 0.38 acres is depicted as a hatched zone in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The hatched area is bounded by Foster Street to the north, the eastern property line to the east, and by dashed delineation lines to the south and west that represent the approximate lateral extent of residual contamination above NCDEQ residential Preliminary Soil Remediation Goals, as determined by the Phase II investigation. The affected area extends approximately 125 feet from the eastern property boundary toward the west and approximately 130 feet from Foster Street toward the south.\n\n**Former UST Location:** The former UST location is marked with a circle-and-cross symbol within the affected area, positioned approximately 40 feet south of Foster Street and approximately 30 feet west of the eastern property boundary.\n\n**Sampling Locations:** Soil boring locations SB-01 through SB-12 are marked with solid circles. Borings SB-05, SB-06, SB-07, SB-08, and SB-09 are clustered within the affected area, while the remaining borings are distributed across the periphery of the affected area and the broader site. Groundwater monitoring well locations MW-01, MW-02, and MW-03 are marked with diamond symbols. MW-02 is located within the affected area, MW-01 is positioned approximately 80 feet southwest of the affected area, and MW-03 is positioned approximately 100 feet southeast along the southern property boundary. Soil gas sampling points SG-01 through SG-08 are marked with triangle symbols within and around the affected area.\n\n**Contamination Delineation:** A dashed line encompasses the approximately 0.38-acre area where residual soil contamination exceeds NCDEQ residential PSRGs for TPH-DRO and/or benzene. This delineation line is based on the interpolation of Phase II soil sampling data and represents the estimated lateral extent of contamination above residential standards.\n\n**Easements:** The 20-foot Duke Energy utility easement along the western boundary of the combined site is depicted as a shaded strip. The stormwater management easement (approximately 0.22 acres) in the southern portion of the site is depicted as a cross-hatched area, with the graded drainage swale and inlet structure indicated.\n\n**Groundwater Flow:** An arrow in the lower right corner of the map indicates the inferred groundwater flow direction toward the southeast.\n\n**Note on Building Footprint:** The following note appears in the map legend: *\\\"The proposed building footprint overlay is not shown on this figure. The client\\'s design team should overlay the proposed building plans with this environmental delineation to confirm the spatial relationship between the affected area and any proposed residential occupancy spaces. Refer to preliminary site plans prepared by Meridian Design Collective PC (drawing set dated September 12, 2024).\\\"*\n\n**[Appendix Reference List]{.underline}**\n\nThe following appendices are included in the full Phase I Environmental Site Assessment report (Sentinel Project No. SEC-2024-1147) but are not included in this excerpted document. Each appendix is available upon request from Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC.\n\n**Appendix A** --- Environmental Database Search Report, prepared by Environmental Data Resources, Inc. (EDR), dated October 10, 2024.\n\n**Appendix B** --- Historical Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps (1901, 1913, 1924, 1937, 1950) for the Subject Property and surrounding area.\n\n**Appendix C** --- Historical Aerial Photographs (1955, 1965, 1972, 1981, 1993, 2005, 2012, 2019).\n\n**Appendix D** --- Regulatory Agency Correspondence and File Review Documentation, including NCDEQ Mooresville Regional Office file review notes, UST Section correspondence, and Durham County Fire Marshal records inquiry.\n\n**Appendix E** --- NCDEQ No Further Action Letter dated September 30, 2024, for Site ID IHSB-24-1203 / Incident No. NONUST-24-0847 (full copy).\n\n**Appendix F** --- Phase II Investigation Report Summary Tables (soil, groundwater, and soil gas analytical results), excerpted from Sentinel Project No. SEC-2024-1089.\n\n**Appendix G** --- Reliance Letter for Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A., dated October 28, 2024.\n\n**Appendix H** --- Site Photographs from October 15, 2024 reconnaissance (27 photographs with descriptions and orientations).\n\n**Appendix I** --- Qualifications of Environmental Professionals (curricula vitae for Dr. James Whitfield, P.E. and Sarah Ng).\n\n**Appendix J** --- Deed Notice Recording...(truncated)", "tool_call_id": "call_00_hffxcVoOU67BJW5Hlbp26441", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/phase-i-esa-excerpt.docx\"}"}
